WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.344 --> 00:00:01.570
(gentle music plays)

2
00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:02.860
Old growth redwoods

3
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.940
that tower 200, 300 feet above the ground,

4
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.883
their root structures only run six to 12 feet deep.

5
00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:19.020
So what holds a redwood up through really rough weather

6
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.750
is not it's trunk that you see,

7
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.250
it's this deep stuff that's underground,

8
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.360
but it's only as deep as it is lateral

9
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.503
because it's held together by a community of other trees.

10
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.200
When the weather is really rough,

11
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.300
what kind of foundation do you have

12
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.120
holding you to the ground?

13
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.963
And these are often qualities that people don't see.

14
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.290
Ambition is a good thing: it's laudable.

15
00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.670
But hustle culture, on the other hand,

16
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:50.640
I think we can all agree

17
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.080
extracts a cost, a cost in the form of exhaustion,

18
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.910
frayed relationships, even substance abuse,

19
00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:02.240
being just a few of the many undesirable side effects of,

20
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.270
of what I would call

21
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.330
an obsessive relationship with performance.

22
00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.660
True success, on the other hand, requires sustainability,

23
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.670
a healthier model for striving, rooted not in compulsion,

24
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.750
but coming from a place of wholeness and love.

25
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.750
Well, here today to elaborate is Brad Stulberg,

26
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.290
a writer and coach

27
00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.670
specializing in human performance and wellbeing.

28
00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.370
Brad is the author of three books,

29
00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.760
including his most recent, "The Practice of Groundedness."

30
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.350
His writing has appeared everywhere

31
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.040
from the New York Times and Wire

32
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.380
to the New Yorker, GQ, and Time.

33
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.620
He is a Fellow

34
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.820
at the University of Michigan's

35
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.630
Graduate School of Public Health,

36
00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.640
and he is the co-founder of, "The Growth Equation,"

37
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.400
newsletter and podcast,

38
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.470
alongside elite track and field coach, Steve Magnus.

39
00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.280
Quick note, before we get into it,

40
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.880
a bit of a trigger warning,

41
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.890
we do discuss a few intense mental health topics,

42
00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.840
including suicidal ideation in fairly graphic detail,

43
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.470
so sensitive viewers, please be advised.

44
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:08.550
That aside, amidst the chaos and the confusion

45
00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.710
that is the self-help world,

46
00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.630
I find Brad to be a fairly principled

47
00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:17.250
and welcome voice of reason as well as a like-minded soul.

48
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.420
And this conversation just brims over

49
00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.420 line:15%
with evidence and experience-based counsel

50
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.190 line:15%
on how to craft successful life with staying power.

51
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.610 line:15%
So without further ado, please hit that subscribe button,

52
00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.393 line:15%
thank you, and enjoy.

53
00:02:32.393 --> 00:02:35.310
Good to see you, my friend. It's been a couple years.

54
00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.900
This is podcast number three,

55
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.640 line:15%
but your first solo appearance on the show.

56
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.160 line:15%
I'm excited to unpack your latest book

57
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.820 line:15%
and all these wisdom tools

58
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.140
that you're so good at sharing with people.

59
00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.030
And every time I think about you or I see you,

60
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:56.030
I remember vividly the first time that we talked.

61
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:57.620
It was for an article

62
00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.300
that I think you were writing for Outside.

63
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:00.490
You called me,

64
00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:01.820
and I remember exactly where I was.

65
00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.717
I was sitting in my truck in this parking lot,

66
00:03:03.717 --> 00:03:06.560
outside this other office that I had, and we ended up,

67
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.550
I thought we were gonna talk for like 10 minutes

68
00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.920
and we ended up talking for like an hour.

69
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.747
And I remember getting off that call and just thinking,

70
00:03:12.747 --> 00:03:14.930
"This guy is like on my wavelength."

71
00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.090
Like we just immediately synced up on these I ideas

72
00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.670
that have gone on to become

73
00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:23.920
the foundation of these books that you write.

74
00:03:25.446 --> 00:03:29.020
And this shared sensibility that I think we have

75
00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:34.020
where we kind of have this bristled resistance

76
00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.193
to the flavor of the month self-help, you know,

77
00:03:37.193 --> 00:03:41.800
that we see kind of shared on social media, et cetera,

78
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.260
a shared allergy to the life hack culture

79
00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.300
and relentless optimization

80
00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.557
and all this kind of reductive, binary,

81
00:03:50.557 --> 00:03:55.061
conclusions and statements about the pursuit of excellence.

82
00:03:55.061 --> 00:03:57.120
So it's good to kind of further that discussion

83
00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.460
and it's nice to be, you know, that that conversation,

84
00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.200
you know, ended up with a friendship

85
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.960
and what we're about to do here today.

86
00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:06.830
So welcome.

87
00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.060
It's great to be here.

88
00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.237
So the latest book is called,

89
00:04:10.237 --> 00:04:11.870
"The Practice of Groundedness."

90
00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.770
So why don't you talk a little bit about

91
00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.470
what inspired you to write this book?

92
00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.530
Because I think the kind of origin story

93
00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:20.610
of what you were enduring and going through that,

94
00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.550
that prompted you to dive into this subject matter

95
00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.050
is super interesting.

96
00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:25.883
Yeah.

97
00:04:26.825 --> 00:04:30.670
So the shortly after

98
00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.660
my first book came out,

99
00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:36.930
that I co-authored with Steve Magnus, "Peak Performance,"

100
00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.960
I was doing some media for the book in New York City

101
00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.920
and went on a long run in Central Park;

102
00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.550
I don't know, 18, 20 miles,

103
00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:47.630
as long as I could go back then.

104
00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:51.140
And was super busy throughout the rest of the day.

105
00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:55.440
So had an interview here, a podcast there,

106
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.540
and just lived on a Cliff Bar.

107
00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.150
And I was under the impression

108
00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.320
that I was meeting a close friend,

109
00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:05.310
a guy named Adam Altar for dinner,

110
00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.490
and turns out it was just a bar.

111
00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:10.870
So I had two stiff drinks and some Kettle Chips,

112
00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.150
and my blood sugar plummeted.

113
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.308
And unbeknownst to me then,

114
00:05:15.308 --> 00:05:17.920
something that happens when your blood sugar plummets

115
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.740
is you can become super jittery and anxious.

116
00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:22.940
And I had a massive panic attack.

117
00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.140
I had never experienced anything like it.

118
00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.710
I felt like I was gonna die.

119
00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:29.330
My heart was pounding out of my neck,

120
00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:30.943
just totally out of control.

121
00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:34.560
And I got through the night, it was rough.

122
00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:35.890
At the time my younger brother

123
00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.510
was finishing up medical school,

124
00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:39.570
so I'm on the phone with him for like two hours.

125
00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.800
And I think he knows immediately, I'm having a panic attack.

126
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:42.910
So fine, "You're fine,"

127
00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.487
but when you're in the middle of that

128
00:05:44.487 --> 00:05:46.350
and you don't believe it.

129
00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:49.360
And the next day I went to an Urgent Care clinic

130
00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:50.450
to get myself checked out

131
00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:52.680
before getting out of flight to go home

132
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.500
and they took my blood sugar.

133
00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:55.333
It was fine then

134
00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:56.166
and the doc there said,

135
00:05:56.166 --> 00:05:58.370
"Yeah, it sounds like your blood sugar got super low.

136
00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.970
You had a panic attack."

137
00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:03.220
And when most people have a panic attack,

138
00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.830
it's a one-and-done kind of thing.

139
00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.420
It's an awful experience; they move on from it.

140
00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.630
For me, it really latched on to my psyche,

141
00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.970
and I just developed this like omnipresent,

142
00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.803
bad anxiety, related to everything health.

143
00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.090
And I got in to see a therapist.

144
00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:23.010
I got in to see my doctor.

145
00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:27.628
They're running all these tests for heart rate-this,

146
00:06:27.628 --> 00:06:30.500
liver-that, adrenal gland-that

147
00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:33.190
ruling out all the health stuff,

148
00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:34.500
and everything's coming back fine,

149
00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.490
but I still don't feel fine.

150
00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:38.170
So for about three weeks

151
00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:42.330
I lived in this place of just terrible health anxiety.

152
00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:46.970
And maybe a month into it, I started feeling a lot better.

153
00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:49.500
And I had seen a therapist a few times.

154
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.150
I was no longer looking for like an organic cause;

155
00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:53.983
just anxiety.

156
00:06:53.983 --> 00:06:55.380
I accepted it.

157
00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:59.330
And I was feeling good enough to go on a long car ride

158
00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:02.583
to meet some close friends for a running and camping trip.

159
00:07:04.127 --> 00:07:07.210
And about an hour and a half into this four hour car ride,

160
00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:10.980
I just got absolutely pummeled by the thought

161
00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.483
that you could just drive off the road and end it.

162
00:07:14.886 --> 00:07:16.800
And it came completely outta left field.

163
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.810
Is that the first instance in your life

164
00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.310
of having any kind of suicidal ideation?

165
00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:22.143
Yes.

166
00:07:22.143 --> 00:07:22.976
Yeah.

167
00:07:22.976 --> 00:07:23.830
Scary.

168
00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:24.663
Terrifying.

169
00:07:24.663 --> 00:07:26.090
And not just the thought,

170
00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:29.460
but the accompaniment of that thought

171
00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.239
with just a head-to-toe wave

172
00:07:32.239 --> 00:07:35.650
of just dread and anxiety, you know,

173
00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.830
stomach coming up into your throat.

174
00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.370
And I couldn't stop thinking about it

175
00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:41.980
for the rest of the drive.

176
00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:43.380
I don't know how I made it there.

177
00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.370
It was just an utterly torturous drive.

178
00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:49.340
And I got there, and then throughout the entire weekend,

179
00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:51.330
I was just constantly bombarded

180
00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:52.520
by these thoughts of self-harm.

181
00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.340
So I'd wake up in the middle of a night to pee

182
00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:55.217
and my brain would be like,

183
00:07:55.217 --> 00:07:56.430
"You could go down in the kitchen

184
00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.390
and stab yourself with a knife."

185
00:07:58.390 --> 00:08:00.890
And it was just freaking unrelenting.

186
00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:04.220
And I didn't let anyone there know what I was going through

187
00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:06.300
because these weren't like my closest friends

188
00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.270
and this was all so very bizarre to me.

189
00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:11.950
And I made it-
Did you call your wife

190
00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:13.987
or your therapist or anything like that?

191
00:08:13.987 --> 00:08:14.820
Yeah.

192
00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.010
I mean, my wife kind of knew,

193
00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.710
but I also didn't wanna scare her, you know?

194
00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:21.103
So I was really holding on to most of this myself

195
00:08:21.103 --> 00:08:22.420
and it was the weekend.

196
00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:23.890
I didn't have my therapist's phone number,

197
00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:24.723
cause as far as she knew,

198
00:08:24.723 --> 00:08:27.060
I was just like a run of the mill case of like some,

199
00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.073
some simple to moderate health anxiety.

200
00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:32.500
Made it through the weekend

201
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:35.780
and was just completely like

202
00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:39.020
white knuckling myself getting ready for the drive home,

203
00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.472
just hoping that it wouldn't happen again,

204
00:08:41.472 --> 00:08:44.580
which the wrong thing to do I learned later,

205
00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:45.750
and of course the whole drive home,

206
00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.410
it's like 10 times as bad as the way there:

207
00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:50.380
by far, the worst four hours of my life,

208
00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.855
just terrible.

209
00:08:52.855 --> 00:08:57.855
Got home, told my wife that something's going on.

210
00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:59.925
I'm sick. Something is wrong with my brain.

211
00:08:59.925 --> 00:09:01.083
I need help.

212
00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.340
I learned that while it's very hard

213
00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:08.190
to get into a psychiatrist if you just have anxiety,

214
00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:10.000
if you have any kind of suicidal ideation

215
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.791
you can get seen immediately the next day.

216
00:09:11.791 --> 00:09:12.624
Yeah.

217
00:09:12.624 --> 00:09:13.457
Right.

218
00:09:13.457 --> 00:09:15.790
So I was, I was seen the next day

219
00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:19.720
and my doctor, Dr. Lucas Van Dyke,

220
00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:21.307
I'll never forget.

221
00:09:21.307 --> 00:09:24.760
I was just terrified and traumatized

222
00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.440
and I'm telling him this story

223
00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:29.100
and that I thought I was actually gonna do it,

224
00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.970
and just how awful it felt,

225
00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:33.900
and I remember asking him,

226
00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.090
saying like, you know, "I need to go

227
00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.410
to be in an inpatient facility somewhere,

228
00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.890
like sign me up for that, cause I don't wanna hurt myself.

229
00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:43.980
I just wanna know I'm safe."

230
00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:45.770
And he's sitting across from me like this

231
00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:47.690
and he just kind of chuckles,

232
00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.187
it's kinda laughing for, and he's like,

233
00:09:49.187 --> 00:09:50.860
"You're not depressed. You're not suicidal.

234
00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:52.057
You just have OCD."

235
00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:56.087
That's such a strange counterintuitive diagnosis.

236
00:09:56.087 --> 00:09:57.180
And at that moment,

237
00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.330
my whole just affect, my shoulders dropped,

238
00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:03.810
it was like, I was free, because I was scared.

239
00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:05.340
I thought that I really was

240
00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:07.940
like going to act on those thoughts.

241
00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:11.287
And over the next year I learned what OCD is

242
00:10:11.287 --> 00:10:14.180
and how it can manifest in different ways.

243
00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:17.260
And one of those manifestations is

244
00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:20.033
having intrusive thoughts about self-harm.

245
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.290
So it was actually not like a bizarre case of OCD.

246
00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:25.673
It's a pretty common theme.

247
00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:27.660
What triggers that?

248
00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:31.080
What is the prompt for suddenly, out of the blue,

249
00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:34.410
being obsessed with a recurring looping thought

250
00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:35.980
of self-harm?

251
00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:39.960
You know, there's no one answer to that question.

252
00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:41.260
Clearly the anxiety

253
00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:43.740
that I was experiencing after the panic attack

254
00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.470
was like priming my brain,

255
00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:49.143
my nervous system, to be on heightened alert.

256
00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.740
But why it went in that direction

257
00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:57.440
and why it happened at that moment, it's hard to say.

258
00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.820
There's some speculation in the research community

259
00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:02.230
that OCD intrusive thoughts

260
00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:05.500
tend to latch onto the things that people care most about.

261
00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:10.110
So people that are really worried about

262
00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.560
or care deeply about like perfectionism,

263
00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:16.500
they tend to have OCD around germs and germ phobia,

264
00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:18.798
because like a germ is gross; it's not perfect.

265
