And the only thing that I would add to that context

1306
00:51:56.858 --> 00:52:01.560
is back to heroic individualism being a cultural issue.

1307
00:52:01.560 --> 00:52:05.200
Everything in the culture tells us not to accept reality.

1308
00:52:05.200 --> 00:52:07.090
You know, you don't feel good.

1309
00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:08.330
There's a substance for that.

1310
00:52:08.330 --> 00:52:09.610
You can go buy some stuff.

1311
00:52:09.610 --> 00:52:11.433
You can tweet. You can post.

1312
00:52:12.499 --> 00:52:15.880
We're constantly kind of like living in this world

1313
00:52:15.880 --> 00:52:18.330
that allows us to distract ourselves

1314
00:52:18.330 --> 00:52:21.030
from whatever the reality might be.

1315
00:52:21.030 --> 00:52:23.810
But of course, if you can't accept where you are,

1316
00:52:23.810 --> 00:52:26.720
then you're never gonna get where you wanna go, right?

1317
00:52:26.720 --> 00:52:29.130
Because you'll be working on the thing

1318
00:52:29.130 --> 00:52:31.800
that isn't actually the thing that you need to be working on

1319
00:52:31.800 --> 00:52:33.490
because you're slightly deluded

1320
00:52:33.490 --> 00:52:35.900
or you're refusing to see it clearly.

1321
00:52:35.900 --> 00:52:38.090
Now a lot of people hear acceptance

1322
00:52:38.090 --> 00:52:41.080
and they think passive resignation, or phoning it in,

1323
00:52:41.080 --> 00:52:42.720
or like, "I'm not gonna try,"

1324
00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:45.340
but that is not at all what acceptance means,

1325
00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:47.910
at least not in the way that I conceptualize it.

1326
00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:50.430
It is actually very empowering because as it tells you,

1327
00:52:50.430 --> 00:52:54.000
here's exactly what's going on, here's the situation I'm in,

1328
00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:55.840
and then that gives you the opportunity

1329
00:52:55.840 --> 00:52:57.990
to take some sort of wise action

1330
00:52:57.990 --> 00:53:00.049
to progress the situation or make it better.

1331
00:53:00.049 --> 00:53:01.100
Right.

1332
00:53:01.100 --> 00:53:03.690
And the more type A and driven you are,

1333
00:53:03.690 --> 00:53:06.750
the more difficult it is for you to reckon with acceptance,

1334
00:53:06.750 --> 00:53:10.200
because you're the person who says, "I do not accept this.

1335
00:53:10.200 --> 00:53:13.040
I am working towards this. This is not okay.

1336
00:53:13.040 --> 00:53:15.580
And I will not stop until this happens."

1337
00:53:15.580 --> 00:53:18.730
And you just plow forward with blinders on.

1338
00:53:18.730 --> 00:53:22.570
And the idea of acceptance is akin to surrender.

1339
00:53:22.570 --> 00:53:24.940
And that's absolutely the last thing

1340
00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:27.150
that I'm going to even entertain.

1341
00:53:27.150 --> 00:53:27.983
Yes.

1342
00:53:29.699 --> 00:53:33.270
And I think that also what can be really helpful

1343
00:53:33.270 --> 00:53:35.470
in less extreme context,

1344
00:53:35.470 --> 00:53:37.610
so yes, surrender in my case, right?

1345
00:53:37.610 --> 00:53:40.407
Trusting Brooke with the knife, and being like,

1346
00:53:40.407 --> 00:53:42.210
"I don't, anything to get me out of here.

1347
00:53:42.210 --> 00:53:44.210
I'm done. I'm lost,"

1348
00:53:44.210 --> 00:53:45.570
Hitting bottom, as someone

1349
00:53:45.570 --> 00:53:48.083
that is suffering with a substance abuse disorder,

1350
00:53:49.430 --> 00:53:52.027
but also just surrendering to reality that,

1351
00:53:52.027 --> 00:53:53.550
"Hey, I'm way too overworked,"

1352
00:53:53.550 --> 00:53:55.660
or, "I'm not living in alignment with my values,"

1353
00:53:55.660 --> 00:53:57.880
or, "This relationship isn't what I thought it would be,"

1354
00:53:57.880 --> 00:54:00.600
or, "I'm not giving enough into this relationship,"

1355
00:54:00.600 --> 00:54:02.490
it's really hard to get there.

1356
00:54:02.490 --> 00:54:07.490
So a couple things that are super concrete and very helpful,

1357
00:54:07.750 --> 00:54:09.610
and they all relate to this concept

1358
00:54:09.610 --> 00:54:11.350
that's called self-distancing.

1359
00:54:11.350 --> 00:54:15.270
So we tend to fuse, "I'm gonna do this for the camera

1360
00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:16.930
so I'm not in front of the microphone here.

1361
00:54:16.930 --> 00:54:19.600
We tend to fuse with our circumstances.

1362
00:54:19.600 --> 00:54:21.240
And when you fuse with your circumstance,

1363
00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:22.800
it's very hard to see it clearly.

1364
00:54:22.800 --> 00:54:24.160
Meaning self-identify.

1365
00:54:24.160 --> 00:54:27.430
Yes, like instead of watching the action movie,

1366
00:54:27.430 --> 00:54:29.340
we're in the action movie.

1367
00:54:29.340 --> 00:54:31.530
And when things are going really well,

1368
00:54:31.530 --> 00:54:32.840
you wanna be in the action movie,

1369
00:54:32.840 --> 00:54:34.690
that's flow, that's a peak experience,

1370
00:54:34.690 --> 00:54:37.970
like you don't wanna be separate from your lived experience,

1371
00:54:37.970 --> 00:54:41.090
but sometimes when things aren't going so great,

1372
00:54:41.090 --> 00:54:42.130
or even if they are,

1373
00:54:42.130 --> 00:54:44.940
it can be really helpful to create some space

1374
00:54:44.940 --> 00:54:46.730
between your awareness of happening

1375
00:54:46.730 --> 00:54:48.640
and what's happening itself.

1376
00:54:48.640 --> 00:54:49.473
Right.

1377
00:54:49.473 --> 00:54:51.430
You did a Twitter thread on this recently,

1378
00:54:51.430 --> 00:54:56.430
you know, a way of translating that is by saying,

1379
00:54:56.630 --> 00:54:58.047
you know, rather than saying,

1380
00:54:58.047 --> 00:54:59.497
"I am depressed," saying,

1381
00:54:59.497 --> 00:55:01.770
"I am somebody who is experiencing depression," right?

1382
00:55:01.770 --> 00:55:03.760
Creating that little gap or that distance

1383
00:55:03.760 --> 00:55:07.690
so you disassociate or, you know,

1384
00:55:07.690 --> 00:55:10.100
deconstruct the self-identification

1385
00:55:10.100 --> 00:55:13.770
and then you're able to evaluate the situation

1386
00:55:13.770 --> 00:55:14.603
that you're in

1387
00:55:14.603 --> 00:55:17.010
and your behavior from a more objective perspective.

1388
00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:17.843
Yes.

1389
00:55:18.765 --> 00:55:22.110
And that is just one of many ways to self-distance.

1390
00:55:22.110 --> 00:55:25.590
Another way that I think is super powerful,

1391
00:55:25.590 --> 00:55:30.440
is this notion of pretending that a really close friend

1392
00:55:30.440 --> 00:55:32.987
is in the same exact situation that you are,

1393
00:55:32.987 --> 00:55:36.660
and really visualizing it down to a T,

1394
00:55:36.660 --> 00:55:38.870
and then giving that friend advice.

1395
00:55:38.870 --> 00:55:39.703
And then you have to

1396
00:55:39.703 --> 00:55:40.705
follow that advice-
We're all so much better

1397
00:55:40.705 --> 00:55:42.624
at that than-
Bingo!

1398
00:55:42.624 --> 00:55:45.840
Or imagine a wiser, older, version of you

1399
00:55:45.840 --> 00:55:47.470
looking back on current you,

1400
00:55:47.470 --> 00:55:49.723
what would wiser, older, you tell you?

1401
00:55:52.051 --> 00:55:53.940
So an extreme example of this,

1402
00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:56.150
but I just freaking love this story

1403
00:55:56.150 --> 00:56:00.590
is, I was working with a woman who's a world-class runner,

1404
00:56:00.590 --> 00:56:03.670
professional runner, multiple time Olympian.

1405
00:56:03.670 --> 00:56:07.930
And she had a proximal high-hamstring strain

1406
00:56:07.930 --> 00:56:10.170
and she was trying to train through it.

1407
00:56:10.170 --> 00:56:14.040
And she's like hobbling down the steps of her apartment

1408
00:56:14.040 --> 00:56:16.913
to go do a mile repeat workout.

1409
00:56:17.780 --> 00:56:20.540
So for the uninitiated, this is like a ball-busting,

1410
00:56:20.540 --> 00:56:23.943
I guess, in her case not so, but a really hard workout.

1411
00:56:25.874 --> 00:56:26.770
And I'm not coaching her on her running,

1412
00:56:26.770 --> 00:56:29.800
I'm there to, you know, provide like mental skill support.

1413
00:56:29.800 --> 00:56:31.220
And I remember saying,

1414
00:56:31.220 --> 00:56:33.547
I'm gonna call her Rachel for the sake of the story,

1415
00:56:33.547 --> 00:56:35.970
"Rachel, imagine that your training partner

1416
00:56:35.970 --> 00:56:37.530
was in this situation,

1417
00:56:37.530 --> 00:56:39.270
and you saw your training partner, Elise,

1418
00:56:39.270 --> 00:56:41.260
hobbling down the stairs,

1419
00:56:41.260 --> 00:56:44.380
kind of grasping at her hamstring on the way out the door

1420
00:56:44.380 --> 00:56:45.490
to do an interval workout.

1421
00:56:45.490 --> 00:56:47.379
What would you tell her?"

1422
00:56:47.379 --> 00:56:49.200
And she's like, "I'd tell her not to do the workout.

1423
00:56:49.200 --> 00:56:50.690
Like she's just gonna blow herself up.

1424
00:56:50.690 --> 00:56:52.403
It's okay to go off the plan.

1425
00:56:52.403 --> 00:56:55.020
I'd tell her it's better to miss a day or two now

1426
00:56:55.020 --> 00:56:56.931
than it is to miss a month later."

1427
00:56:56.931 --> 00:56:57.764
Like,

1428
00:56:57.764 --> 00:57:00.150
well then why on earth are you going to do this workout?

1429
00:57:00.150 --> 00:57:01.690
Cause you don't understand Brad.

1430
00:57:01.690 --> 00:57:03.356
Well, no.
I can make this work.

1431
00:57:03.356 --> 00:57:04.630
Yes, and, I think that

1432
00:57:04.630 --> 00:57:05.920
when you give that advice to someone,

1433
00:57:05.920 --> 00:57:07.123
you very quickly see that,

1434
00:57:07.123 --> 00:57:09.045
like you can't make it work.

1435
00:57:09.045 --> 00:57:10.730
This isn't a coaching session,

1436
00:57:10.730 --> 00:57:12.730
but in your situation with like feeling ungrounded

1437
00:57:12.730 --> 00:57:15.410
and you being like, "Well, I have these competing values.

1438
00:57:15.410 --> 00:57:18.090
It's really hard to know which one to prioritize."

1439
00:57:18.090 --> 00:57:19.200
I think in that instance,

1440
00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:22.400
if you could imagine yourself 30 years down the road,

1441
00:57:22.400 --> 00:57:25.170
looking back on current you, in that situation,

1442
00:57:25.170 --> 00:57:27.490
it might give you some insight for at least for today

1443
00:57:27.490 --> 00:57:29.250
like, "Hey, here's the decision that I should make."

1444
00:57:29.250 --> 00:57:30.083
Yeah.

1445
00:57:30.083 --> 00:57:32.370
It's not bulletproof, but, again, what it does

1446
00:57:32.370 --> 00:57:35.320
is it takes this, I'm fused with the situation.

1447
00:57:35.320 --> 00:57:36.450
I'm in an Olympic cycle.

1448
00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:38.560
I need to train. It's on my plan.

1449
00:57:38.560 --> 00:57:40.520
I'm so caught up in the storm

1450
00:57:40.520 --> 00:57:42.790
and it puts you here so that you can watch the storm

1451
00:57:42.790 --> 00:57:44.100
and then gives you a chance to make

1452
00:57:44.100 --> 00:57:44.933
a more thought-
Right.

1453
00:57:44.933 --> 00:57:46.410
thoughtful decision.

1454
00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:48.030
On the subject of acceptance,

1455
00:57:48.030 --> 00:57:51.160
the path towards acceptance is paved with pain, right?

1456
00:57:51.160 --> 00:57:52.770
Pain is like the great catalyst

1457
00:57:52.770 --> 00:57:55.350
for this awakened self-awareness,

1458
00:57:55.350 --> 00:57:56.253
Often.

1459
00:57:56.253 --> 00:57:58.760
and the ability to develop acceptance

1460
00:57:58.760 --> 00:58:00.760
around what's occurring.

1461
00:58:00.760 --> 00:58:01.593
In your case,

1462
00:58:01.593 --> 00:58:05.140
it was a much more severe case of OCD than I realized.

1463
00:58:05.140 --> 00:58:07.720
In my case, it was grappling with addiction,

1464
00:58:07.720 --> 00:58:10.270
and you know, later in life with some health stuff.

1465
00:58:11.620 --> 00:58:16.010
Short of experiencing some kind of extreme, you know,

1466
00:58:16.010 --> 00:58:17.830
emotional experience or,

1467
00:58:17.830 --> 00:58:21.633
you know what we would call a bottom in recovery,

1468
00:58:24.170 --> 00:58:27.530
you can grow into this level of acceptance

1469
00:58:27.530 --> 00:58:28.770
around whatever it is you're doing.

1470
00:58:28.770 --> 00:58:29.700
It's just harder, right?

1471
00:58:29.700 --> 00:58:33.050
Like pain really makes it, as painful as it is,

1472
00:58:33.050 --> 00:58:35.463
it actually makes this whole process easier.

1473
00:58:35.463 --> 00:58:36.296
Yes.

1474
00:58:36.296 --> 00:58:37.910
It's like an, it, it creates an opportunity

1475
00:58:37.910 --> 00:58:40.660
or it helps that door swing wide open.

1476
00:58:40.660 --> 00:58:44.780
And I'm interested in the coaching that you do with people,

1477
00:58:44.780 --> 00:58:47.270
like they're probably not hitting a bottom.

1478
00:58:47.270 --> 00:58:48.510
They're probably high performers

1479
00:58:48.510 --> 00:58:51.760
who are just trying to, hone their game a little bit more,

1480
00:58:51.760 --> 00:58:52.800
maybe are experiencing

1481
00:58:52.800 --> 00:58:54.960
some things that aren't working in their life.

1482
00:58:54.960 --> 00:58:55.880
But you know,

1483
00:58:55.880 --> 00:58:58.445
they're not bottoming out on drugs and alcohol or,

1484
00:58:58.445 --> 00:59:01.350
you know, having some, suicidal ideation.

1485
00:59:01.350 --> 00:59:02.573
So how do you coach somebody

1486
00:59:02.573 --> 00:59:05.163
towards a greater level of acceptance

1487
00:59:05.163 --> 00:59:06.930
who's not in that position?

1488
00:59:06.930 --> 00:59:08.310
I mean, you just gave an example,

1489
00:59:08.310 --> 00:59:11.070
but what's another way that you would think about that?

1490
00:59:11.070 --> 00:59:11.920
Hmm.

1491
00:59:11.920 --> 00:59:14.260
Well, another great example is

1492
00:59:14.260 --> 00:59:17.060
just having a coach, or

1493
00:59:17.060 --> 00:59:20.700
in the case of someone that may not have access to that

1494
00:59:20.700 --> 00:59:23.670
is other people that you can trust, right?

1495
00:59:23.670 --> 00:59:25.890
Because if you can't see the world clearly

1496
00:59:25.890 --> 00:59:27.970
well go ask someone that you trust and,

1497
00:59:27.970 --> 00:59:29.280
it's gotta be someone that is willing

1498
00:59:29.280 --> 00:59:31.354
to tell you-
the real feedback.

1499
00:59:31.354 --> 00:59:32.897
The real deal.

1500
00:59:32.897 --> 00:59:37.007
So it's just ways to get outside of yourself a little bit.

1501
00:59:37.007 --> 00:59:39.030
And I do think that, yes,

1502
00:59:39.030 --> 00:59:42.193
deep pain can be a gateway to acceptance.

1503
00:59:43.180 --> 00:59:45.130
No one gets through life unscathed:

1504
00:59:45.130 --> 00:59:46.830
everyone experiences some pain.

1505
00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:47.663
And I'll tell you what,

1506
00:59:47.663 --> 00:59:51.160
like before I got sick with OCD, I thought like, you know,

1507
00:59:51.160 --> 00:59:53.650
getting dumped by my college girlfriend was pain

1508
00:59:53.650 --> 00:59:56.580
or having a book flop was pain.

1509
00:59:56.580 --> 01:00:01.060
Like in, based on my reference point, that was real pain.

1510
01:00:01.060 --> 01:00:03.400
So as a coach, when someone comes to you

1511
01:00:03.400 --> 01:00:04.970
with what they're experiencing is pain,

1512
01:00:04.970 --> 01:00:06.520
I have to be totally nonjudgmental

1513
01:00:06.520 --> 01:00:08.970
because I might be thinking like, "That's not real pain."

1514
01:00:08.970 --> 01:00:10.720
But to that person, it is.

1515
01:00:10.720 --> 01:00:13.250
So anytime that you meet a barrier,

1516
01:00:13.250 --> 01:00:15.300
there's some suffering, or there's some tension,

1517
01:00:15.300 --> 01:00:18.417
or sense of disconnect, I think is a pathway to explore,

1518
01:00:18.417 --> 01:00:20.460
"Well, what's really going on here?

1519
01:00:20.460 --> 01:00:22.710
What's the actual problem that we're trying to solve?"

1520
01:00:22.710 --> 01:00:23.543
Right.

1521
01:00:23.543 --> 01:00:25.060
And reframing pain

1522
01:00:25.060 --> 01:00:27.820
or whatever difficult experience you're enduring

1523
01:00:27.820 --> 01:00:29.110
as an opportunity,

1524
01:00:29.110 --> 01:00:30.520
like where is,

1525
01:00:30.520 --> 01:00:33.530
where is the learning experience in all of this to grow?

1526
01:00:33.530 --> 01:00:34.363
Yeah.

1527
01:00:34.363 --> 01:00:35.430
The only thing I'd say there

1528
01:00:35.430 --> 01:00:37.340
is, back to non-dual thinking,

1529
01:00:37.340 --> 01:00:38.940
I wouldn't say as an opportunity, but,

1530
01:00:38.940 --> 01:00:42.460
and an opportunity, because pain sucks.

1531
01:00:42.460 --> 01:00:45.280
And like, I'm not one to paint suffering as positive;

1532
01:00:45.280 --> 01:00:46.620
like suffering sucks.

1533
01:00:46.620 --> 01:00:49.330
Like the less that you suffer, the better.

1534
01:00:49.330 --> 01:00:53.900
And suffering exists, and, it's gonna happen inevitably,

1535
01:00:53.900 --> 01:00:55.490
so when it happens, yes,

1536
01:00:55.490 --> 01:00:57.780
there's also an opportunity along with it.

1537
01:00:57.780 --> 01:00:58.613
Right.

1538
01:00:58.613 --> 01:00:59.860
Can you look at it as a,
It's both.

1539
01:00:59.860 --> 01:01:00.693
as a teacher?

1540
01:01:00.693 --> 01:01:03.280
Yeah, both and, like this sucks, and it's a teacher.

1541
01:01:03.280 --> 01:01:07.890
And sometimes that has to happen on its own

1542
01:01:07.890 --> 01:01:10.483
and it has to happen later, because one of the worst things

1543
01:01:10.483 --> 01:01:12.370
that you can do when you're in pain

1544
01:01:12.370 --> 01:01:15.570
is put this another expectation on yourself to like,

1545
01:01:15.570 --> 01:01:16.460
it has to be meaningful.

1546
01:01:16.460 --> 01:01:18.126
Yeah. That's a good point.
And I did this,

1547
01:01:18.126 --> 01:01:18.959
I'm A type a pusher.

1548
01:01:18.959 --> 01:01:20.680
I remember, you know, I don't know, three,

1549
01:01:20.680 --> 01:01:23.037
four months into my recovery, being like,

1550
01:01:23.037 --> 01:01:24.570
"I don't feel like I'm growing from this

1551
01:01:24.570 --> 01:01:26.380
and it has to be meaningful.

1552
01:01:26.380 --> 01:01:28.660
And I don't think I'm ever gonna write about this."

1553
01:01:28.660 --> 01:01:31.870
At the time, and again, I remember my therapist, Brooke,

1554
01:01:31.870 --> 01:01:34.570
looking at me and being like, "Why the hell do you care?

1555
01:01:34.570 --> 01:01:36.280
Like this doesn't have to be meaningful.

1556
01:01:36.280 --> 01:01:39.300
You have severe OCD. It just sucks.

1557
01:01:39.300 --> 01:01:40.447
Can that be enough?"

1558
01:01:41.350 --> 01:01:43.740
And of course, now, a couple years later,

1559
01:01:43.740 --> 01:01:45.480
we're talking about it, I did write a book:

1560
01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:46.430
it's super meaningful.

1561
01:01:46.430 --> 01:01:47.690
It's probably, you know,

1562
01:01:47.690 --> 01:01:49.890
shy of like falling in love and having a kid,

1563
01:01:49.890 --> 01:01:52.220
it's by far, maybe even on the same level,

1564
01:01:52.220 --> 01:01:53.690
most meaningful experience in my life.

1565
01:01:53.690 --> 01:01:56.800
But when I was in it, utterly meaningless.

1566
01:01:56.800 --> 01:01:57.633
That means,
and that's okay.

1567
01:01:57.633 --> 01:02:00.650
the meaning isn't revealed immediately.

1568
01:02:00.650 --> 01:02:01.800
It's only in time.

1569
01:02:01.800 --> 01:02:02.633
Right.

1570
01:02:02.633 --> 01:02:06.190
And the psychologist, Daniel Gilbert talks about

1571
01:02:06.190 --> 01:02:09.300
like how we all have a psychological immune system

1572
01:02:09.300 --> 01:02:10.630
and you can't fake it.

1573
01:02:10.630 --> 01:02:12.340
So when you're really suffering, if you're like, "Oh,

1574
01:02:12.340 --> 01:02:14.390
I'm gonna grow from this," your psychological immune

1575
01:02:14.390 --> 01:02:15.440
system's like.

1576
01:02:15.440 --> 01:02:18.520
Like, "We just need to fight this and get rid of this pain."

1577
01:02:18.520 --> 01:02:20.620
But then afterwards, like the real immune system,

1578
01:02:20.620 --> 01:02:21.453
it gets stronger.

1579
01:02:21.453 --> 01:02:23.070
Like an immune system doesn't get stronger

1580
01:02:23.070 --> 01:02:25.760
as it's fighting the thing, it gets stronger after.

1581
01:02:25.760 --> 01:02:26.593
So yes,

1582
01:02:26.593 --> 01:02:29.720
sometimes you can reframe pain as like a growth opportunity.

1583
01:02:29.720 --> 01:02:33.210
And that might be the pain of a minor business failure,

1584
01:02:33.210 --> 01:02:34.990
or something in a friendship,

1585
01:02:34.990 --> 01:02:37.310
or a creative endeavor gone sour,

1586
01:02:37.310 --> 01:02:41.300
but illness, death, loss, I often think

1587
01:02:41.300 --> 01:02:43.610
that it's actually just okay to meet it where it is,

1588
01:02:43.610 --> 01:02:46.780
and then let the meaning and growth happen on its own time.

1589
01:02:46.780 --> 01:02:49.700
Which leads into the next pillar here, which is presence.

1590
01:02:49.700 --> 01:02:52.040
Like, can you just be present with what is happening?

1591
01:02:52.040 --> 01:02:52.873
Yes.

1592
01:02:52.873 --> 01:02:54.515
In acceptance, and present with it.

1593
01:02:54.515 --> 01:02:57.311
I don't know if I can. And it depends on the day.

1594
01:02:57.311 --> 01:02:58.179
(Brad laughing)

1595
01:02:58.179 --> 01:02:59.012
It's pretty tough.

1596
01:02:59.012 --> 01:03:00.519
It's tough.

1597
01:03:00.519 --> 01:03:03.890
And yet I'm gonna, I'm gonna kind of, I'm gonna get real

1598
01:03:05.130 --> 01:03:07.023
and in the moment on this table,

1599
01:03:08.026 --> 01:03:10.680
so I'm as present as I've been,

1600
01:03:10.680 --> 01:03:13.116
I don't know, in the last week, right now?

1601
01:03:13.116 --> 01:03:15.913
What's not on this table?

1602
01:03:17.490 --> 01:03:18.750
What do you mean?

1603
01:03:18.750 --> 01:03:20.940
There's no cell phone, right?

1604
01:03:20.940 --> 01:03:22.690
There's no Twitter.

1605
01:03:22.690 --> 01:03:23.523
No.

1606
01:03:23.523 --> 01:03:24.550
If I need to write something down,

1607
01:03:24.550 --> 01:03:26.836
I've got a notebook and a pen.

1608
01:03:26.836 --> 01:03:31.836
I think that we think of presence as this internal quality,

1609
01:03:32.240 --> 01:03:35.260
which it can be, but it's also very external.

1610
01:03:35.260 --> 01:03:37.130
So a huge part of presence

1611
01:03:37.130 --> 01:03:39.560
is putting yourself in situations

1612
01:03:39.560 --> 01:03:41.060
where it's easy to be present.

1613
01:03:42.310 --> 01:03:44.320
I often think that presence happens

1614
01:03:44.320 --> 01:03:47.790
upstream of the moment that you wanna be present.

1615
01:03:47.790 --> 01:03:49.350
I know you recently had Johan Hari on.

1616
01:03:49.350 --> 01:03:50.920
That was a great episode.

1617
01:03:50.920 --> 01:03:53.520
And he talked about like telling someone not to itch

1618
01:03:53.520 --> 01:03:56.020
I think it was, if like you pour itching sauce on them

1619
01:03:56.020 --> 01:03:58.540
and like the distractions in our life's the itching sauce.

1620
01:03:58.540 --> 01:04:00.173
So back to core values,

1621
01:04:01.290 --> 01:04:03.190
if one of your core values is presence

1622
01:04:03.190 --> 01:04:04.930
or related to things that you care about,

1623
01:04:04.930 --> 01:04:07.650
well, then you have to be pretty intentional

1624
01:04:07.650 --> 01:04:10.840
about setting up the time, and space, and conditions,

1625
01:04:10.840 --> 01:04:12.110
to be present.

1626
01:04:12.110 --> 01:04:13.940
Something else that I talk about all the time

1627
01:04:13.940 --> 01:04:16.450
with coaching clients that wanna be more present

1628
01:04:16.450 --> 01:04:20.640
is there's a reason that monks that achieve enlightenment;

1629
01:04:20.640 --> 01:04:22.263
they're all in monasteries.

1630
01:04:23.490 --> 01:04:24.690
Cultivating an environment

1631
01:04:24.690 --> 01:04:26.270
that's conducive to-
Yeah, like,

1632
01:04:26.270 --> 01:04:27.103
being present,
Yes.

1633
01:04:27.103 --> 01:04:28.890
But what about the relationship

1634
01:04:28.890 --> 01:04:31.640
between presence and groundedness?

1635
01:04:31.640 --> 01:04:33.690
Particularly in the context of like

1636
01:04:33.690 --> 01:04:36.290
working with very driven people,

1637
01:04:36.290 --> 01:04:37.990
presence feels like an indulgence,

1638
01:04:37.990 --> 01:04:41.100
it feels like an obstacle in the way of achieving

1639
01:04:41.100 --> 01:04:44.390
whatever you need to get done on a particular day

1640
01:04:44.390 --> 01:04:46.370
so you have to sell the bill of goods

1641
01:04:46.370 --> 01:04:47.870
as to why this is important.

1642
01:04:47.870 --> 01:04:49.030
Time out.

1643
01:04:49.030 --> 01:04:52.870
You said on a particular day, and that's the thing,

1644
01:04:52.870 --> 01:04:57.870
deep presence, and really like honing that

1645
01:04:57.870 --> 01:04:59.380
is never going to get you

1646
01:04:59.380 --> 01:05:02.600
the most work done on a particular day, ever,

1647
01:05:02.600 --> 01:05:04.430
but it might over the a course of a year,

1648
01:05:04.430 --> 01:05:06.810
or a decade, or a career, and the,

1649
01:05:06.810 --> 01:05:07.643
The tortoise,

1650
01:05:07.643 --> 01:05:08.476
the tortoise over the hare.

1651
01:05:08.476 --> 01:05:09.309
Yes.

1652
01:05:09.309 --> 01:05:10.720
And that's how you settle that score, right there.

1653
01:05:10.720 --> 01:05:12.750
I'm super clear with my coaching clients.

1654
01:05:12.750 --> 01:05:13.780
They come to me talking about

1655
01:05:13.780 --> 01:05:16.470
efficiency, and productivity, and optimization,

1656
01:05:16.470 --> 01:05:17.670
and I'm like, "All these things are great.

1657
01:05:17.670 --> 01:05:20.390
I want all these things too, but on what time scale?"

1658
01:05:20.390 --> 01:05:23.560
Because if I wanted to optimize over the next 24 hours,

1659
01:05:23.560 --> 01:05:25.550
or even over the next week let's say,

1660
01:05:25.550 --> 01:05:28.080
I would slam espresso and Red Bulls,

1661
01:05:28.080 --> 01:05:29.640
I'd sleep four hours a night,

1662
01:05:29.640 --> 01:05:32.876
and I would just bury myself and that'd be super optimal,

1663
01:05:32.876 --> 01:05:36.301
but not for a month, certainly not for a year.

1664
01:05:36.301 --> 01:05:38.797
So I think that there's a zooming out and saying,

1665
01:05:38.797 --> 01:05:41.800
"Hey, like what timescale are we operating on?"

1666
01:05:41.800 --> 01:05:43.820
And there, the research is unequivocal.

1667
01:05:43.820 --> 01:05:45.840
If you're talking about excellence or success

1668
01:05:45.840 --> 01:05:47.480
over the long haul

1669
01:05:47.480 --> 01:05:49.814
being present for the work that actually matters

1670
01:05:49.814 --> 01:05:53.550
is perhaps the most important variable

1671
01:05:53.550 --> 01:05:56.810
that you can do to set yourself up for that success.

1672
01:05:56.810 --> 01:05:57.643
Right.

1673
01:05:57.643 --> 01:06:01.670
We don't intuitively think about these horizons

1674
01:06:01.670 --> 01:06:03.580
in decade long chunks,

1675
01:06:03.580 --> 01:06:06.500
we think about the next quarterly earnings,

1676
01:06:06.500 --> 01:06:10.220
or the next six months are perhaps the next year

1677
01:06:10.220 --> 01:06:12.170
but if you really want to build sustainability,

1678
01:06:12.170 --> 01:06:14.756
which is what is at the core of what this is all about

1679
01:06:14.756 --> 01:06:15.589
like,
A hundred percent.

1680
01:06:15.589 --> 01:06:17.200
how do you wanna operate to the best of your ability

1681
01:06:17.200 --> 01:06:19.030
for the longest period of time?

1682
01:06:19.030 --> 01:06:21.270
You have to broaden that window, right?

1683
01:06:21.270 --> 01:06:24.090
And I've had to learn this, like I'm, you know,

1684
01:06:24.090 --> 01:06:25.810
I just want to grind and grind and grind.

1685
01:06:25.810 --> 01:06:28.560
And I've been doing this podcast for almost 10 years.

1686
01:06:28.560 --> 01:06:30.130
It became pretty clear like two years ago,

1687
01:06:30.130 --> 01:06:31.980
like I'm not gonna make it,

1688
01:06:31.980 --> 01:06:34.840
like I have to figure out a different way of doing this

1689
01:06:34.840 --> 01:06:37.480
so that I can maintain my joy for it

1690
01:06:37.480 --> 01:06:39.870
and my presence when I'm sitting with guests

1691
01:06:39.870 --> 01:06:42.340
and my enthusiasm for, you know,

1692
01:06:42.340 --> 01:06:44.990
consuming all of their wisdom and material

1693
01:06:44.990 --> 01:06:48.790
and being able to deliver on the best experience,

1694
01:06:48.790 --> 01:06:50.910
conversational experience that I can,

1695
01:06:50.910 --> 01:06:52.950
I can't sustain it the way I was doing it.

1696
01:06:52.950 --> 01:06:55.000
So now I take a month off every year,

1697
01:06:55.000 --> 01:06:58.630
I've hired a team and like, I've, I've had to learn how to,

1698
01:06:58.630 --> 01:07:02.880
you know, overcome this perfectionism and control impulses.

1699
01:07:02.880 --> 01:07:04.480
And those were not easy lessons for me.

1700
01:07:04.480 --> 01:07:08.120
And I'm not great at them every day either,

1701
01:07:08.120 --> 01:07:10.810
but it was like a necessity, I think,

1702
01:07:10.810 --> 01:07:13.270
in order to do that which gets into,

1703
01:07:13.270 --> 01:07:14.600
we're sort of moving out of presence

1704
01:07:14.600 --> 01:07:17.490
and more into the next thing, which is, which is patience.

1705
01:07:17.490 --> 01:07:19.231
Yeah. And I think built into patience

1706
01:07:19.231 --> 01:07:21.700
is this idea of looking at things, you know,

1707
01:07:21.700 --> 01:07:23.280
on a longer timeframe.

1708
01:07:23.280 --> 01:07:24.113
Yeah.

1709
01:07:24.113 --> 01:07:25.661
Real quick: I jotted a note down.

1710
01:07:25.661 --> 01:07:27.850
Did you, during that period,

1711
01:07:27.850 --> 01:07:29.070
and we don't have to name (indistinct)

1712
01:07:29.070 --> 01:07:31.140
but did you ever find yourself resenting

1713
01:07:31.140 --> 01:07:33.730
the fact that you had to come in and talk to someone?

1714
01:07:33.730 --> 01:07:36.160
It never got that bad, but I was moving in that direction.

1715
01:07:36.160 --> 01:07:39.140
And, and I really never wanted to feel that way.

1716
01:07:39.140 --> 01:07:41.730
And I realized that if I didn't change some things

1717
01:07:41.730 --> 01:07:44.420
that I was quickly gonna be that person.

1718
01:07:44.420 --> 01:07:47.270
And obviously, that's at odds with

1719
01:07:47.270 --> 01:07:49.726
the experience that I'm trying to cultivate and create.

1720
01:07:49.726 --> 01:07:53.120
I asked that because that emotion is often,

1721
01:07:53.120 --> 01:07:56.350
in my coaching clients, a game changer

1722
01:07:56.350 --> 01:07:57.695
for helping people realize that

1723
01:07:57.695 --> 01:08:02.380
their work style is too frenetic and frantic,

1724
01:08:02.380 --> 01:08:05.550
too "optimized," on short time scales,

1725
01:08:05.550 --> 01:08:09.860
is, people come to resent work that they once loved,

1726
01:08:09.860 --> 01:08:12.590
and the only thing that's changed about the work

1727
01:08:12.590 --> 01:08:14.610
is that when they started doing it,

1728
01:08:14.610 --> 01:08:17.730
they were a team of one and they had a really low bar

1729
01:08:17.730 --> 01:08:20.800
and they spent most of their day deep in the creative work,

1730
01:08:20.800 --> 01:08:23.110
and now suddenly they're running meetings

1731
01:08:23.110 --> 01:08:24.370
and they're leading teams,

1732
01:08:24.370 --> 01:08:28.030
and that deep focus, present work has gone away

1733
01:08:28.030 --> 01:08:29.920
and they start to resent their job.

1734
01:08:29.920 --> 01:08:31.990
And the number one thing to help those people,

1735
01:08:31.990 --> 01:08:35.530
are to help them reclaim those moments of presence.

1736
01:08:35.530 --> 01:08:36.740
And I'll say one more thing on this

1737
01:08:36.740 --> 01:08:37.780
before moving on to patience,

1738
01:08:37.780 --> 01:08:41.670
cause I think it's a very important coaching insight

1739
01:08:41.670 --> 01:08:45.480
and that is that it unequivocally you will fail

1740
01:08:45.480 --> 01:08:47.800
if you just say, "I wanna be more present."

1741
01:08:47.800 --> 01:08:51.030
And even if you try to work top down, you'll likely fail.

1742
01:08:51.030 --> 01:08:53.210
So to say like, "I need to be present all the time,"

1743
01:08:53.210 --> 01:08:55.000
it's never gonna work; there's too many distractions.

1744
01:08:55.000 --> 01:08:58.200
So with clients, I always have folks work bottom up.

1745
01:08:58.200 --> 01:09:01.730
So that means just start with two hours a week.

1746
01:09:01.730 --> 01:09:04.630
So schedule ahead of time, two, one hour blocks

1747
01:09:04.630 --> 01:09:06.890
where you know what you're gonna do during that period

1748
01:09:06.890 --> 01:09:09.428
so when it rolls around, you don't just pull up your email

1749
01:09:09.428 --> 01:09:10.890
and just start there.

1750
01:09:10.890 --> 01:09:13.360
And then after two weeks, try an hour and a half.

1751
01:09:13.360 --> 01:09:17.100
And I had this one client who came to me

1752
01:09:17.100 --> 01:09:19.540
because of feeling resentment and just like

1753
01:09:20.430 --> 01:09:22.890
coming to hate a job that she loved.

1754
01:09:22.890 --> 01:09:23.830
And at first I'm like, yeah,

1755
01:09:23.830 --> 01:09:26.750
maybe it's the job actually, and she needs to move on,

1756
01:09:26.750 --> 01:09:27.850
but it quickly became clear

1757
01:09:27.850 --> 01:09:29.030
that she actually loved the work,

1758
01:09:29.030 --> 01:09:32.380
she just didn't love how she was running around frantically.

1759
01:09:32.380 --> 01:09:34.320
And she said, there's no way she could be present.

1760
01:09:34.320 --> 01:09:36.150
She's high up. She runs this huge team.

1761
01:09:36.150 --> 01:09:38.204
She's got kids, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

1762
01:09:38.204 --> 01:09:40.100
And we just started with an hour a day

1763
01:09:40.100 --> 01:09:43.040
and slowly but surely, she proved to herself

1764
01:09:43.040 --> 01:09:44.340
that the world wasn't gonna end

1765
01:09:44.340 --> 01:09:48.710
if for an hour a day, she put everything away and worked.

1766
01:09:48.710 --> 01:09:51.960
And a year into our coaching relationship,

1767
01:09:51.960 --> 01:09:53.100
this is a true story

1768
01:09:53.100 --> 01:09:55.270
I, and I try to practice what I preach.

1769
01:09:55.270 --> 01:09:56.453
So I am very,

1770
01:09:57.500 --> 01:09:59.090
I have boundaries around when I coach.

1771
01:09:59.090 --> 01:10:02.065
It's like certain days, certain times. And I went to try to

1772
01:10:02.065 --> 01:10:04.290
work with her executive assistant to get a coaching slot,

1773
01:10:04.290 --> 01:10:07.260
And the person's like, "No, like, sorry,

1774
01:10:07.260 --> 01:10:09.810
like Kate can't meet with you then, her afternoons

1775
01:10:09.810 --> 01:10:11.310
are all blocked off.
her window

1776
01:10:11.310 --> 01:10:12.143
and your window didn't,

1777
01:10:12.143 --> 01:10:13.760
So she went from impossible to be present

1778
01:10:13.760 --> 01:10:16.790
at a company with over a thousand people

1779
01:10:16.790 --> 01:10:19.840
to three and a half to four hours a day.

1780
01:10:19.840 --> 01:10:20.673
But it started,

1781
01:10:20.673 --> 01:10:21.890
if I would've told her that at the beginning,

1782
01:10:21.890 --> 01:10:22.880
she would've fired me.

1783
01:10:22.880 --> 01:10:24.850
And I think the same is true with ourselves.

1784
01:10:24.850 --> 01:10:26.510
Even if you don't have a coach, individually,

1785
01:10:26.510 --> 01:10:27.343
if you tell yourself,

1786
01:10:27.343 --> 01:10:29.888
"Oh, I'm gonna be present for four hours," and you fail,

1787
01:10:29.888 --> 01:10:31.030
it's like a diet.

1788
01:10:31.030 --> 01:10:32.450
It's like anything, when you're off the wagon,

1789
01:10:32.450 --> 01:10:33.780
it's hard to get back on.

1790
01:10:33.780 --> 01:10:36.710
So I think starting really small, and again,

1791
01:10:36.710 --> 01:10:38.987
it's a common theme in this book,

1792
01:10:38.987 --> 01:10:41.730
and I think this is the coach in me, is that

1793
01:10:41.730 --> 01:10:44.040
you take something that's really noble and aspirational,

1794
01:10:44.040 --> 01:10:45.040
but for it to work,

1795
01:10:45.040 --> 01:10:47.110
you've gotta get super concrete

1796
01:10:47.110 --> 01:10:50.199
and start at a level that is like in the weeds.

1797
01:10:50.199 --> 01:10:52.720
So I just wanted to add that in.

1798
01:10:52.720 --> 01:10:53.553
Yeah.

1799
01:10:53.553 --> 01:10:55.130
I think, in furtherance of that idea

1800
01:10:55.130 --> 01:10:58.080
is the notion of just being really granular.

1801
01:10:58.080 --> 01:11:00.380
You're gonna cultivate an environment

1802
01:11:00.380 --> 01:11:02.730
that's conducive to you being present,

1803
01:11:02.730 --> 01:11:05.190
but what does presence actually look like?

1804
01:11:05.190 --> 01:11:08.580
In sobriety, it's talked about in the context of

1805
01:11:09.760 --> 01:11:11.430
what is the next right action?

1806
01:11:11.430 --> 01:11:13.830
Like if you're just fully present with yourself,

1807
01:11:14.830 --> 01:11:18.160
meaning how do you overcome the overwhelm

1808
01:11:18.160 --> 01:11:21.470
of all these different competing stressors

1809
01:11:21.470 --> 01:11:22.303
that are in our mind?

1810
01:11:22.303 --> 01:11:23.700
"Oh, I have to do that. I gotta do that.

1811
01:11:23.700 --> 01:11:25.130
How am I gonna solve that problem?"

1812
01:11:25.130 --> 01:11:26.950
Okay. What's the next right action?

