2073
01:21:08.720 --> 01:21:11.400
And it requires a lot of confidence and security

2074
01:21:11.400 --> 01:21:13.530
cause the worst managers are super insecure

2075
01:21:13.530 --> 01:21:16.600
and they think they always need to be doing stuff, right?

2076
01:21:16.600 --> 01:21:18.420
No different than probably a helicopter parent

2077
01:21:18.420 --> 01:21:19.750
that like is insecure if

2078
01:21:19.750 --> 01:21:21.110
their kid's not gonna turn out great

2079
01:21:21.110 --> 01:21:23.038
if they don't do everything.

2080
01:21:23.038 --> 01:21:23.871
Right.

2081
01:21:23.871 --> 01:21:25.600
Or an individual working on a project?

2082
01:21:25.600 --> 01:21:27.160
Like generally overdoing something

2083
01:21:27.160 --> 01:21:29.130
is a sign of our own insecurity.

2084
01:21:29.130 --> 01:21:31.160
It's the athlete that in her case,

2085
01:21:31.160 --> 01:21:33.630
has to go out and run a really hard workout

2086
01:21:33.630 --> 01:21:35.630
to prove to herself that she's still fit,

2087
01:21:35.630 --> 01:21:37.430
that's born out of insecurity.

2088
01:21:37.430 --> 01:21:40.370
So I think that also part of being a good manager

2089
01:21:40.370 --> 01:21:42.970
is having the security to know that, "Hey, I don't,

2090
01:21:42.970 --> 01:21:46.170
there's no visible observable concrete thing

2091
01:21:46.170 --> 01:21:48.090
I necessarily need to do.

2092
01:21:48.090 --> 01:21:49.020
I just need to, again,

2093
01:21:49.020 --> 01:21:51.040
define the at space and then hold it

2094
01:21:51.040 --> 01:21:53.460
for my team to do their thing."

2095
01:21:53.460 --> 01:21:55.360
And that's where just like just the word,

2096
01:21:55.360 --> 01:21:56.260
like, "Be good enough,"

2097
01:21:56.260 --> 01:21:58.260
has been so powerful with clients

2098
01:21:58.260 --> 01:21:59.730
cause it serves as this little cue

2099
01:21:59.730 --> 01:22:01.368
to step back and ask themselves,

2100
01:22:01.368 --> 01:22:03.010
"Like what are we really trying to do here?"

2101
01:22:03.010 --> 01:22:05.030
One of the challenges with patience,

2102
01:22:05.030 --> 01:22:08.459
if you're somebody who is ambitious,

2103
01:22:08.459 --> 01:22:12.100
you're inevitably going to be comparing your,

2104
01:22:12.100 --> 01:22:14.850
your trajectory to other people, right?

2105
01:22:14.850 --> 01:22:17.687
And, and here's Brad, who's telling you,

2106
01:22:17.687 --> 01:22:20.230
"Slow it down a little bit, be present,

2107
01:22:20.230 --> 01:22:22.050
be patient, life is long,

2108
01:22:22.050 --> 01:22:24.679
don't worry about what those other people are doing."

2109
01:22:24.679 --> 01:22:26.750
It's easier to say that

2110
01:22:26.750 --> 01:22:30.210
than to actually embody that and practice that.

2111
01:22:30.210 --> 01:22:33.670
So, how do you help people disconnect

2112
01:22:33.670 --> 01:22:37.440
from that kind of external motivation or pressure

2113
01:22:38.906 --> 01:22:41.060
of comparing yourself to others?

2114
01:22:41.060 --> 01:22:42.690
Back to the core values.

2115
01:22:42.690 --> 01:22:44.850
So all these things are entangled, right?

2116
01:22:44.850 --> 01:22:46.670
So then you go look at your core values

2117
01:22:46.670 --> 01:22:48.520
and how you define them and how you practice them

2118
01:22:48.520 --> 01:22:49.537
and just say like, "Well,

2119
01:22:49.537 --> 01:22:53.147
what would living my core values be in this moment?"

2120
01:22:54.756 --> 01:22:56.550
And then you just do it, you know,

2121
01:22:56.550 --> 01:22:58.060
behavioral activation, like you,

2122
01:22:58.060 --> 01:22:59.330
you don't have, in that moment,

2123
01:22:59.330 --> 01:23:00.950
you probably don't feel like you should slow down.

2124
01:23:00.950 --> 01:23:03.830
You probably feel like you should speed up.

2125
01:23:03.830 --> 01:23:04.983
But I think the core values

2126
01:23:04.983 --> 01:23:07.400
like that becomes your internal dashboard.

2127
01:23:07.400 --> 01:23:10.500
So ideally you're not spending as much time

2128
01:23:10.500 --> 01:23:12.680
comparing yourself to everyone else,

2129
01:23:12.680 --> 01:23:14.609
you're spending more time comparing yourself

2130
01:23:14.609 --> 01:23:18.630
against your core values and measuring yourself that way,

2131
01:23:18.630 --> 01:23:23.576
and then letting that guide your actions and your behaviors.

2132
01:23:23.576 --> 01:23:25.180
It's a tough shift.

2133
01:23:25.180 --> 01:23:30.100
I mean we moved from Northern California, big city, airport,

2134
01:23:30.100 --> 01:23:33.090
tons of opportunities to talk about books and sell books

2135
01:23:33.090 --> 01:23:34.990
and build a coaching practice,

2136
01:23:34.990 --> 01:23:38.340
to a small mountain town in Western North Carolina.

2137
01:23:38.340 --> 01:23:39.260
And it was tough

2138
01:23:39.260 --> 01:23:42.070
and that was a hundred percent a values-based decision.

2139
01:23:42.070 --> 01:23:45.710
But in the moment, that move was like, "What am I doing?

2140
01:23:45.710 --> 01:23:46.930
Like I'm not gonna sell as many books."

2141
01:23:46.930 --> 01:23:49.170
But like it, it made not, it didn't it,

2142
01:23:49.170 --> 01:23:52.510
the inertia of the moment and speed did not make sense,

2143
01:23:52.510 --> 01:23:54.330
but from a values perspective, it made sense.

2144
01:23:54.330 --> 01:23:57.200
And it didn't feel great making that decision,

2145
01:23:57.200 --> 01:24:00.090
but now that we've done it, it actually feels really good

2146
01:24:00.090 --> 01:24:01.130
but there's a real trade off.

2147
01:24:01.130 --> 01:24:04.730
Would I be "more successful" externally

2148
01:24:04.730 --> 01:24:06.800
if I still lived in the Bay Area?

2149
01:24:06.800 --> 01:24:09.020
Maybe, probably at this young age,

2150
01:24:09.020 --> 01:24:12.550
but would I be successfully against my internal dashboard?

2151
01:24:12.550 --> 01:24:13.830
Probably not.

2152
01:24:13.830 --> 01:24:14.663
Right.

2153
01:24:14.663 --> 01:24:16.290
Well, the counter argument would be

2154
01:24:16.290 --> 01:24:20.390
that by living in a smaller town, there's less distractions.

2155
01:24:20.390 --> 01:24:23.180
So the actual work of writing the books

2156
01:24:23.180 --> 01:24:26.210
and the frequency of these books coming out

2157
01:24:26.210 --> 01:24:27.400
is gonna be enhanced.

2158
01:24:27.400 --> 01:24:31.120
And ultimately that's the larger lever to your success.

2159
01:24:31.120 --> 01:24:33.800
If you're in Oakland, maybe you can go on more podcasts.

2160
01:24:33.800 --> 01:24:35.820
Maybe you can talk about it a little bit more

2161
01:24:35.820 --> 01:24:39.490
and be more engaged with media and promotion.

2162
01:24:39.490 --> 01:24:41.740
But ultimately, over the long run,

2163
01:24:41.740 --> 01:24:44.910
the thing that matters most is the quality of the work

2164
01:24:44.910 --> 01:24:47.760
and creating an environment that's conducive to that

2165
01:24:47.760 --> 01:24:52.000
is the key thing that aligns the values with, you know,

2166
01:24:52.000 --> 01:24:54.959
the actual like byproduct of your actions.

2167
01:24:54.959 --> 01:24:55.792
A hundred percent.

2168
01:24:55.792 --> 01:24:57.630
And you said long run like two times in that sentence.

2169
01:24:57.630 --> 01:24:59.670
Cause again, it's patience: it's playing the long game.

2170
01:24:59.670 --> 01:25:04.280
But I didn't get there, like out of this esoteric value,

2171
01:25:04.280 --> 01:25:07.390
I got there because my wife and I said, "What do we about?

2172
01:25:07.390 --> 01:25:09.240
We care about nature. We care about family.

2173
01:25:09.240 --> 01:25:10.381
We care about love.

2174
01:25:10.381 --> 01:25:11.214
Well what would it look

2175
01:25:11.214 --> 01:25:12.450
like to practice those things in Oakland

2176
01:25:12.450 --> 01:25:13.940
versus in a smaller town?"

2177
01:25:13.940 --> 01:25:18.852
And it was a very like somewhat rational decision

2178
01:25:18.852 --> 01:25:22.191
that now it has this huge emotional benefit, which is,

2179
01:25:22.191 --> 01:25:23.930
I feel like I can play the long game.

2180
01:25:23.930 --> 01:25:25.500
You'd think I'd get more writing done,

2181
01:25:25.500 --> 01:25:26.742
but I walk out the door,

2182
01:25:26.742 --> 01:25:28.830
hit the trails with my German Shepherd.

2183
01:25:28.830 --> 01:25:31.700
If anything, I'm a professional walker now.

2184
01:25:31.700 --> 01:25:33.270
Do a lot of long walks.

2185
01:25:33.270 --> 01:25:36.560
Without any audio? Just solo?

2186
01:25:36.560 --> 01:25:37.513
It depends.

2187
01:25:39.244 --> 01:25:40.930
I go back and forth.

2188
01:25:40.930 --> 01:25:42.530
Sometimes I'll listen to your podcast

2189
01:25:42.530 --> 01:25:44.900
because I don't really commute so it is my time

2190
01:25:44.900 --> 01:25:46.860
if I'm gonna listen to a podcast.

2191
01:25:46.860 --> 01:25:49.770
Sometimes I'll listen to like Vivaldi or classical music.

2192
01:25:49.770 --> 01:25:50.760
I listen to Dispatch,

2193
01:25:50.760 --> 01:25:53.190
who I was so shocked that you hadn't heard of.

2194
01:25:53.190 --> 01:25:55.110
I'm an old man, Brad, come on, give me a break.

2195
01:25:55.110 --> 01:25:55.943
Those guys are old.

2196
01:25:55.943 --> 01:25:58.070
I listen to REM, and, Radiohead,

2197
01:25:58.070 --> 01:26:00.520
and like a couple other bands and that's like it.

2198
01:26:00.520 --> 01:26:02.640
I'm stuck in my ways.

2199
01:26:02.640 --> 01:26:04.980
Yeah. Dispatch, came a little bit later, but not much.

2200
01:26:04.980 --> 01:26:06.447
I bet those guys are like 49, you know,

2201
01:26:06.447 --> 01:26:07.650
they're peaking right now.

2202
01:26:07.650 --> 01:26:10.800
Their songs are so wise. Go check 'em out.

2203
01:26:10.800 --> 01:26:11.633
I'll check them.

2204
01:26:11.633 --> 01:26:15.740
But yeah, so, so, so yes, it all loops back.

2205
01:26:15.740 --> 01:26:17.810
Like all these things are integrated and in that case,

2206
01:26:17.810 --> 01:26:20.670
the patience and the core values go hand in hand.

2207
01:26:20.670 --> 01:26:22.270
But if you don't have that internal dashboard,

2208
01:26:22.270 --> 01:26:25.030
then you're gonna be sucked up into the rat race,

2209
01:26:25.030 --> 01:26:27.590
into measurement, into speed.

2210
01:26:27.590 --> 01:26:29.700
And it's not either/or,

2211
01:26:29.700 --> 01:26:32.380
most people live in both these worlds every day,

2212
01:26:32.380 --> 01:26:34.180
maybe even every hour.

2213
01:26:34.180 --> 01:26:36.270
So the game is just trying to, again,

2214
01:26:36.270 --> 01:26:39.080
realize when you're in the world that you don't wanna be in

2215
01:26:39.080 --> 01:26:41.730
and then have some tools to get you back on the path.

2216
01:26:42.690 --> 01:26:46.020
One of those tools being the practice of vulnerability,

2217
01:26:46.020 --> 01:26:50.080
we talked earlier about you, you know,

2218
01:26:50.080 --> 01:26:51.191
writing about your OCD

2219
01:26:51.191 --> 01:26:56.180
and how important engaging in that vulnerability has been

2220
01:26:56.180 --> 01:26:59.623
in your journey and in your process.

2221
01:26:59.623 --> 01:27:00.668
But why is this like

2222
01:27:00.668 --> 01:27:03.673
one of the core principles of groundedness?

2223
01:27:05.080 --> 01:27:09.000
Well, I don't think that you can truly be strong

2224
01:27:09.000 --> 01:27:10.843
unless you are vulnerable.

2225
01:27:11.900 --> 01:27:16.900
And Brene Brown writes beautifully about vulnerability

2226
01:27:18.087 --> 01:27:21.300
and in particular, the benefits

2227
01:27:21.300 --> 01:27:23.653
of connecting with other people.

2228
01:27:24.942 --> 01:27:27.350
So, there's no point of me talking about that.

2229
01:27:27.350 --> 01:27:29.699
Y'all could just go listen to, read, Brene Brown.

2230
01:27:29.699 --> 01:27:34.540
Something that she doesn't speak about as frequently

2231
01:27:34.540 --> 01:27:38.030
is the benefits of being vulnerable with yourself,

2232
01:27:38.030 --> 01:27:40.143
for your ability to trust yourself.

2233
01:27:42.583 --> 01:27:45.070
And that is why vulnerability makes you stronger.

2234
01:27:45.070 --> 01:27:50.070
If you have parts of yourself that you don't explore,

2235
01:27:51.780 --> 01:27:53.763
that you're scared to explore,

2236
01:27:55.348 --> 01:27:58.860
that you kind of know are there, but you don't go there,

2237
01:27:58.860 --> 01:28:03.570
those parts of yourself tend to creep up and uproot you,

2238
01:28:03.570 --> 01:28:06.460
to stick to the groundedness metaphor.

2239
01:28:06.460 --> 01:28:08.510
There's this ancient Greek myth

2240
01:28:08.510 --> 01:28:12.520
that I just love about the god Pan.

2241
01:28:12.520 --> 01:28:17.060
And Pan lived just outside of the village boundaries.

2242
01:28:17.060 --> 01:28:20.780
And every once in a while, people would be

2243
01:28:20.780 --> 01:28:25.330
out hunting or foraging and they'd get lost

2244
01:28:25.330 --> 01:28:27.920
and they'd venture into the terrain of Pan,

2245
01:28:27.920 --> 01:28:29.860
and then they'd see Pan.

2246
01:28:29.860 --> 01:28:33.440
And he was so terrifying that as they ran away,

2247
01:28:33.440 --> 01:28:35.913
they would freeze outta fear and die.

2248
01:28:37.060 --> 01:28:40.690
Whereas in the myth, a few courageous villagers,

2249
01:28:40.690 --> 01:28:43.880
deliberately ventured out to go see Pan

2250
01:28:43.880 --> 01:28:46.060
and to the people that went to see him,

2251
01:28:46.060 --> 01:28:49.363
Pan bestowed upon them, wisdom, compassion, and strength.

2252
01:28:50.440 --> 01:28:51.660
And I read that

2253
01:28:51.660 --> 01:28:54.700
as just a beautiful metaphor for vulnerability.

2254
01:28:54.700 --> 01:28:57.840
So we all have our Pans, right?

2255
01:28:57.840 --> 01:28:59.100
These are like our cracks,

2256
01:28:59.100 --> 01:29:01.260
the parts of us that make us fragile.

2257
01:29:01.260 --> 01:29:02.540
And if we try to avoid them,

2258
01:29:02.540 --> 01:29:04.331
eventually we're gonna stumble into them

2259
01:29:04.331 --> 01:29:05.663
and it's gonna be ugly.

2260
01:29:05.663 --> 01:29:09.180
And there's a lot of projection that happens too I think.

2261
01:29:09.180 --> 01:29:11.370
Whereas if we can face them,

2262
01:29:11.370 --> 01:29:13.800
sometimes with the help of other people,

2263
01:29:13.800 --> 01:29:17.000
a therapist, a coach, more often than not a friend,

2264
01:29:17.000 --> 01:29:19.120
community members, family,

2265
01:29:19.120 --> 01:29:21.080
it actually makes us stronger.

2266
01:29:21.080 --> 01:29:24.560
Because it's very hard to trust all of yourself,

2267
01:29:24.560 --> 01:29:26.894
unless you fully know all of yourself.

2268
01:29:26.894 --> 01:29:27.740
Right.

2269
01:29:27.740 --> 01:29:30.150
But short of some kind of crisis,

2270
01:29:30.150 --> 01:29:33.100
it's easier to just live another day,

2271
01:29:33.100 --> 01:29:35.040
compartmentalizing all of those things

2272
01:29:35.040 --> 01:29:37.576
and not look at those things, rather than

2273
01:29:37.576 --> 01:29:40.640
continue to peel back the layer and peel back the layer.

2274
01:29:40.640 --> 01:29:42.210
And it's like, "Is this ever gonna end?"

2275
01:29:42.210 --> 01:29:45.310
There's no end to the amount of stuff

2276
01:29:45.310 --> 01:29:48.167
that's down there waiting to be excavated, Brad.

2277
01:29:48.167 --> 01:29:49.335
Yes/and-
It's exhausting-

2278
01:29:49.335 --> 01:29:52.210
And, it's back to both/and,

2279
01:29:52.210 --> 01:29:55.129
And? You, you, did you take improv classes?

2280
01:29:55.129 --> 01:29:56.180
No, why? Is that a thing?

2281
01:29:56.180 --> 01:29:57.593
Yes/and, is like the core of improv.

2282
01:29:57.593 --> 01:29:58.426
Really?
Yeah,

2283
01:29:58.426 --> 01:29:59.870
you do yes/and to everything.

2284
01:29:59.870 --> 01:30:00.973
Oh I love that/it.

2285
01:30:00.973 --> 01:30:02.700
You never say no, when get a prompt,

2286
01:30:02.700 --> 01:30:06.530
and I've noticed you, in this practice of yes/and,

2287
01:30:06.530 --> 01:30:07.950
which is good for conversation.

2288
01:30:07.950 --> 01:30:09.800
Yeah. I never knew it was an improv thing.

2289
01:30:09.800 --> 01:30:11.530
I always think of it, it's non-dual thinking

2290
01:30:11.530 --> 01:30:13.380
because I'm a firm believer that like

2291
01:30:13.380 --> 01:30:16.350
very few things are either/or; they tend to be both/and.

2292
01:30:16.350 --> 01:30:18.340
Or like in this context, versus that.

2293
01:30:18.340 --> 01:30:22.260
How do you survive Twitter with that, with that ideology?

2294
01:30:22.260 --> 01:30:23.210
I schedule a lot of my

2295
01:30:23.210 --> 01:30:24.370
tweets-
I say that as a joke,

2296
01:30:24.370 --> 01:30:28.340
like, no, somebody who appreciates nuance

2297
01:30:28.340 --> 01:30:30.334
and can see, you know,

2298
01:30:30.334 --> 01:30:31.167
The Twitter-
the Twitter

2299
01:30:31.167 --> 01:30:32.990
complexities of everything, the Twitter thread.

2300
01:30:32.990 --> 01:30:34.510
Cause Twitter's like your main thing.

2301
01:30:34.510 --> 01:30:37.410
Yeah. The Twitter thread function is nice now.

2302
01:30:37.410 --> 01:30:38.570
Like I do a lot more threads

2303
01:30:38.570 --> 01:30:40.550
where I actually explain things.

2304
01:30:40.550 --> 01:30:43.360
And Twitter is just, I mean, I'm being, and being raw.

2305
01:30:43.360 --> 01:30:44.330
I probably shouldn't say this.

2306
01:30:44.330 --> 01:30:46.080
Like Twitter is just a gateway

2307
01:30:46.080 --> 01:30:49.240
to have people hopefully discover my books, because like

2308
01:30:49.240 --> 01:30:50.240
that's where I really have

2309
01:30:50.240 --> 01:30:52.423
the time and space to explore this.

2310
01:30:53.380 --> 01:30:55.200
But a lot of people live there

2311
01:30:55.200 --> 01:30:57.003
so that's where I meet them.

2312
01:30:57.890 --> 01:31:00.663
But to your point about like how to do all of this?

2313
01:31:01.530 --> 01:31:02.363
Nondual.

2314
01:31:02.363 --> 01:31:05.210
Like the, if you, you were to spend your whole day

2315
01:31:05.210 --> 01:31:07.500
just peeling back that layer

2316
01:31:08.510 --> 01:31:10.320
and exploring your own mortality

2317
01:31:10.320 --> 01:31:12.893
and just all these things that are really hard,

2318
01:31:13.760 --> 01:31:15.950
you'd be like one of these existentialists

2319
01:31:15.950 --> 01:31:17.793
that ends up quite miserable.

2320
01:31:18.840 --> 01:31:22.120
But totally denying those things, also, I don't think,

2321
01:31:22.120 --> 01:31:24.330
is a ticket to a rich and textured life.

2322
01:31:24.330 --> 01:31:26.570
And you can't run away from them forever anyways.

2323
01:31:26.570 --> 01:31:28.420
Like eventually they catch up to you.

2324
01:31:28.420 --> 01:31:32.373
So it's about knowing them without harping on them.

2325
01:31:33.910 --> 01:31:37.840
And I think that most people by trying not to know them,

2326
01:31:37.840 --> 01:31:40.027
again, at some point like you come head to head with them

2327
01:31:40.027 --> 01:31:45.000
and that's when it becomes really hard to detach.

2328
01:31:45.000 --> 01:31:46.903
Yeah, the,

2329
01:31:49.120 --> 01:31:51.080
the war that I wage

2330
01:31:51.080 --> 01:31:54.370
is understanding these principles

2331
01:31:54.370 --> 01:31:55.687
and I practice vulnerability.

2332
01:31:55.687 --> 01:31:57.910
And I understand how that makes you

2333
01:31:59.900 --> 01:32:01.800
more able to connect with other people.

2334
01:32:01.800 --> 01:32:03.867
Like, I think there's this notion like,

2335
01:32:03.867 --> 01:32:05.460
"Oh, I'll make friends by, you know,

2336
01:32:05.460 --> 01:32:07.490
presenting the best version of myself

2337
01:32:07.490 --> 01:32:09.530
and those people will be impressed,

2338
01:32:09.530 --> 01:32:11.760
and they'll invite me into their friend group,"

2339
01:32:11.760 --> 01:32:13.430
when, of course it's the opposite.

2340
01:32:13.430 --> 01:32:16.920
It's only when you're honest and you can disarm people

2341
01:32:16.920 --> 01:32:21.013
with your level of like candor and self-reflection,

2342
01:32:22.960 --> 01:32:25.990
that's really the glue I think.

2343
01:32:25.990 --> 01:32:27.600
And I think I'm pretty good at that.

2344
01:32:27.600 --> 01:32:28.810
But I think what I do

2345
01:32:28.810 --> 01:32:33.810
is I fall into this pattern of tomorrow.

2346
01:32:34.540 --> 01:32:37.320
Like I know that like I gotta get through today.

2347
01:32:37.320 --> 01:32:38.940
I know all this groundedness stuff,

2348
01:32:38.940 --> 01:32:41.910
but like I got 10 things I gotta get through.

2349
01:32:41.910 --> 01:32:44.620
So I just can't like take that moment

2350
01:32:44.620 --> 01:32:47.270
to do that other thing that I know is good for me

2351
01:32:47.270 --> 01:32:50.460
and that's just gonna have to wait for another day.

2352
01:32:50.460 --> 01:32:53.147
And then that day becomes two days, becomes three days.

2353
01:32:53.147 --> 01:32:53.980
Yeah.

2354
01:32:53.980 --> 01:32:56.530
I mean, you say this on all your podcasts, man.

2355
01:32:56.530 --> 01:32:58.020
Like, "You just have to do it."

2356
01:32:58.020 --> 01:33:00.890
So it's back to like scheduling it, owning it,

2357
01:33:00.890 --> 01:33:04.400
not waiting, not needing to feel motivated to do it.

2358
01:33:04.400 --> 01:33:06.860
There might never be a good time,

2359
01:33:06.860 --> 01:33:07.803
but that doesn't mean

2360
01:33:07.803 --> 01:33:11.240
that you shouldn't put it on your calendar and do it.

2361
01:33:11.240 --> 01:33:12.630
And in this case, I don't know what that might be.

2362
01:33:12.630 --> 01:33:14.940
It might be like structured contemplation.

2363
01:33:14.940 --> 01:33:19.355
It might be biweekly session with a therapist.

2364
01:33:19.355 --> 01:33:20.211
It might be

2365
01:33:20.211 --> 01:33:24.130
scheduling time with a close friend to just dive deep.

2366
01:33:24.130 --> 01:33:26.040
I think that you sell yourself short.

2367
01:33:26.040 --> 01:33:28.850
I think that you get a lot of that in your work

2368
01:33:28.850 --> 01:33:30.960
because you get to have these conversations right now.

2369
01:33:30.960 --> 01:33:34.530
Like what are at least 70% of your podcast shows

2370
01:33:34.530 --> 01:33:36.810
if not peeling back

2371
01:33:36.810 --> 01:33:37.780
those layers?
Well, they're

2372
01:33:37.780 --> 01:33:39.064
self-serving in that regard

2373
01:33:39.064 --> 01:33:39.897
for sure-
But you

2374
01:33:39.897 --> 01:33:42.090
built it into your life, so you do do it.

2375
01:33:42.090 --> 01:33:42.923
I, I do.

2376
01:33:44.649 --> 01:33:45.572
And, it is true.

2377
01:33:45.572 --> 01:33:46.733
Like last night I had a therapy group meeting

2378
01:33:46.733 --> 01:33:50.350
that I have here every Monday night with a bunch of dudes.

2379
01:33:50.350 --> 01:33:52.810
But that means that I'm up later

2380
01:33:53.871 --> 01:33:55.640
and I don't get as much sleep.

2381
01:33:55.640 --> 01:33:58.540
And then today, because I had that last night,

2382
01:33:58.540 --> 01:34:00.840
I wasn't as prepared to talk to you today.

2383
01:34:00.840 --> 01:34:03.130
So I made decision to block out the morning

2384
01:34:03.130 --> 01:34:05.640
to make sure I was optimally prepared to talk to you.

2385
01:34:05.640 --> 01:34:07.580
Which meant that I didn't train today.

2386
01:34:07.580 --> 01:34:09.930
So there's so many moving pieces.

2387
01:34:09.930 --> 01:34:12.690
And for me it's about just being okay with the fact

2388
01:34:12.690 --> 01:34:13.730
that like, I'm not gonna get

2389
01:34:13.730 --> 01:34:16.330
every piece in the right place every single day.

2390
01:34:16.330 --> 01:34:17.400
And that's okay.

2391
01:34:17.400 --> 01:34:20.473
So not feed myself?
like transcend that,

2392
01:34:21.555 --> 01:34:23.118
that default setting

2393
01:34:23.118 --> 01:34:24.680
that is telling me that I failed

2394
01:34:24.680 --> 01:34:26.820
because I didn't do these three things

2395
01:34:26.820 --> 01:34:28.440
that I would've preferred to have done

2396
01:34:28.440 --> 01:34:30.470
because I had these other competing values

2397
01:34:30.470 --> 01:34:32.950
that took precedence on this particular day.

2398
01:34:32.950 --> 01:34:34.190
So what you just said to me

2399
01:34:34.190 --> 01:34:36.580
is a beautiful and very concrete example

2400
01:34:36.580 --> 01:34:40.130
of the self-compassion and self-discipline.

2401
01:34:40.130 --> 01:34:43.770
Because it takes a lot of self-discipline to do what you do.

2402
01:34:43.770 --> 01:34:45.540
But if you didn't have that self-compassion too,

2403
01:34:45.540 --> 01:34:46.373
you'd burn out.

2404
01:34:46.373 --> 01:34:47.680
Like imagine if you went into this,

2405
01:34:47.680 --> 01:34:49.147
and I'm sure there's still a part of you that's like,

2406
01:34:49.147 --> 01:34:50.790
"Damn it Rich, like you're off your schedule.

2407
01:34:50.790 --> 01:34:51.660
You didn't train,"

2408
01:34:51.660 --> 01:34:52.493
whatever-
Right.

2409
01:34:52.493 --> 01:34:53.326
I was like, "Oh, I should have

2410
01:34:53.326 --> 01:34:56.160
just woken up earlier and made sure I got my training in

2411
01:34:56.160 --> 01:34:57.517
and then I could have also done this."

2412
01:34:57.517 --> 01:35:00.580
But then, I have done that also,

2413
01:35:00.580 --> 01:35:02.920
but then I'm rushing from one thing to the next.

2414
01:35:02.920 --> 01:35:04.900
And yeah, I can check all those boxes,

2415
01:35:04.900 --> 01:35:07.070
but I'm less present for all of those things.

2416
01:35:07.070 --> 01:35:10.060
And I'm actually less fulfilled and happy

2417
01:35:10.060 --> 01:35:13.280
because it's always about, you know,

2418
01:35:13.280 --> 01:35:14.920
being on this ticking clock

2419
01:35:14.920 --> 01:35:17.830
and making sure that I get from point A to point B on time.

2420
01:35:17.830 --> 01:35:20.063
Another thing that I just noticed, and again, I know,

2421
01:35:20.063 --> 01:35:21.500
I know we're not coaching here,

2422
01:35:21.500 --> 01:35:24.047
is the word should is always a flag for me.

2423
01:35:24.047 --> 01:35:25.710
I say that a lot.

2424
01:35:25.710 --> 01:35:26.543
Yeah.

2425
01:35:26.543 --> 01:35:27.593
I know you-
Yeah.

2426
01:35:27.593 --> 01:35:28.426
talk about that so explain that.

2427
01:35:28.426 --> 01:35:30.163
Yeah. Don't should all over yourself.

2428
01:35:31.390 --> 01:35:32.830
When you catch yourself-
I constantly do that.

2429
01:35:32.830 --> 01:35:35.040
When you catch yourself shoulding, ask yourself,

2430
01:35:35.040 --> 01:35:37.173
can you replace it with a wish or a want?

2431
01:35:38.400 --> 01:35:39.923
And if the answer is no,

2432
01:35:41.523 --> 01:35:44.490
then maybe you shouldn't do that thing.

2433
01:35:44.490 --> 01:35:48.580
And if the answer is yes, then even that softens the tone.

2434
01:35:48.580 --> 01:35:51.520
So, "I wish I would've been able to exercise."

2435
01:35:51.520 --> 01:35:53.693
It's not nearly as judgemental.

2436
01:35:55.370 --> 01:36:00.370
There's a parable in Buddhism about the second arrow.

2437
01:36:00.720 --> 01:36:02.720
I love this too. I mean, I love all these parables,

2438
01:36:02.720 --> 01:36:04.270
cause there's so much truth in them.

2439
01:36:04.270 --> 01:36:05.960
And the, it goes like this.

2440
01:36:05.960 --> 01:36:08.830
The first arrow is something that we can't control

2441
01:36:08.830 --> 01:36:10.700
and it can be internal or external.

2442
01:36:10.700 --> 01:36:11.970
I used to think it was just external,

2443
01:36:11.970 --> 01:36:13.040
but OCD will teach you

2444
01:36:13.040 --> 01:36:15.924
that you cannot really control your thoughts.

2445
01:36:15.924 --> 01:36:17.870
So you could have an intrusive thought,

2446
01:36:17.870 --> 01:36:20.137
your schedule could get away from you, whatever it is.

2447
01:36:20.137 --> 01:36:22.720
And the first arrow hurts. It sucks.

2448
01:36:22.720 --> 01:36:25.320
But the second arrow, the third arrow, the fourth arrow,

2449
01:36:25.320 --> 01:36:27.000
all those additional arrows,

2450
01:36:27.000 --> 01:36:29.500
are your judgements about those things,

2451
01:36:29.500 --> 01:36:30.600
your repression of those things,

2452
01:36:30.600 --> 01:36:33.120
your shoulding on yourself.

2453
01:36:33.120 --> 01:36:36.570
And those arrows often hurt worse than the first arrow.

2454
01:36:36.570 --> 01:36:39.100
Right? It's the compound interest of negativity.

2455
01:36:39.100 --> 01:36:39.933
Yes.
Right?

2456
01:36:39.933 --> 01:36:40.766
Like you make the mistake

2457
01:36:40.766 --> 01:36:42.630
and then you beat yourself up for making the mistake

2458
01:36:42.630 --> 01:36:44.340
so you've just made a second mistake.

2459
01:36:44.340 --> 01:36:46.203
Yes. And you can't be perfect.

2460
01:36:47.640 --> 01:36:49.270
Again though, nondual.

2461
01:36:49.270 --> 01:36:50.917
Like, it doesn't mean that you should say,

2462
01:36:50.917 --> 01:36:52.580
"Well to hell with it all."

2463
01:36:52.580 --> 01:36:55.690
Or, "Let's all just hold hands and sing 'Kumbaya,'"

2464
01:36:55.690 --> 01:36:57.580
it means that, "Let's hold hands and sing 'Kumbaya,'"

2465
01:36:57.580 --> 01:36:59.180
and accept that we're not perfect."

2466
01:36:59.180 --> 01:37:01.910
And let's identify things that matter for us

2467
01:37:01.910 --> 01:37:04.403
and try to get better in, in stress.

2468
01:37:05.910 --> 01:37:09.220
Lot of like mid-century, George Leonard,

2469
01:37:09.220 --> 01:37:11.460
before him, Alan Watts, these kind of

2470
01:37:11.460 --> 01:37:15.970
self-fashioned gurus and philosophers of the good life,

2471
01:37:15.970 --> 01:37:18.430
many of whom have had a lot of good things to say,

2472
01:37:18.430 --> 01:37:21.393
they often talked about the wisdom of insecurity,

2473
01:37:22.290 --> 01:37:25.208
and that's an Alan Watts' term in particular.

2474
01:37:25.208 --> 01:37:29.460
And just how like accepting that you're not perfect

2475
01:37:29.460 --> 01:37:31.210
and being okay with that

2476
01:37:31.210 --> 01:37:33.890
is actually the key to getting better.

2477
01:37:33.890 --> 01:37:35.370
Because if you constantly hold yourself

2478
01:37:35.370 --> 01:37:37.140
to this bar of perfect,

2479
01:37:37.140 --> 01:37:40.030
like you're gonna just getting your own way all the time.

2480
01:37:40.030 --> 01:37:42.280
So that's another example of this nondual thinking

2481
01:37:42.280 --> 01:37:45.420
you can be both good enough, and get better.

2482
01:37:45.420 --> 01:37:47.490
You can be both loving to yourself,

2483
01:37:47.490 --> 01:37:48.953
and really hard on yourself.

2484
01:37:50.370 --> 01:37:51.960
On the subject of vulnerability,

2485
01:37:51.960 --> 01:37:54.440
I'm just putting myself in the shoes of,

2486
01:37:54.440 --> 01:37:58.440
let's say an executive at a corporation.

2487
01:37:58.440 --> 01:38:02.400
And he is listening to this, or she is listening to this

2488
01:38:02.400 --> 01:38:04.210
and thinking, "Okay, I understand that,

2489
01:38:04.210 --> 01:38:07.200
but I can't show vulnerability in the workplace.

2490
01:38:07.200 --> 01:38:08.910
That's gonna be the death knell.

2491
01:38:08.910 --> 01:38:10.610
My whole thing is to make sure

2492
01:38:10.610 --> 01:38:13.120
that everybody understands that I'm in charge.

2493
01:38:13.120 --> 01:38:15.720
I've got everything under control,

2494
01:38:15.720 --> 01:38:19.301
and that's how we, you know, push this train forward."

2495
01:38:19.301 --> 01:38:21.300
That's a really tricky situation.

2496
01:38:21.300 --> 01:38:22.380
And-
and I'm sure you

2497
01:38:22.380 --> 01:38:24.046
hear that a lot.
Yeah. It's a common one.

2498
01:38:24.046 --> 01:38:24.879
Yeah.

2499
01:38:24.879 --> 01:38:27.093
And the coaching tool that I use there is,

2500
01:38:28.017 --> 01:38:30.483
"Ask yourself what you really wanna say,

2501
01:38:31.400 --> 01:38:34.710
and then say something as close to that as possible."

2502
01:38:34.710 --> 01:38:36.750
So the there's a story here.

2503
01:38:36.750 --> 01:38:40.450
And one of my first coaching clients

2504
01:38:40.450 --> 01:38:43.580
was a woman who in the book I called Donna,

2505
01:38:43.580 --> 01:38:44.622
not her real name.

2506
01:38:44.622 --> 01:38:48.580
And she called herself the accidental executive.

2507
01:38:48.580 --> 01:38:52.160
So she had no goal of being an executive,

2508
01:38:52.160 --> 01:38:54.380
and sure enough, there she found herself

2509
01:38:54.380 --> 01:38:55.970
in the number three position

2510
01:38:55.970 --> 01:39:00.970
at a multinational organization with over 50,000 employees.

2511
01:39:02.100 --> 01:39:06.900
And she was the only woman on the 20th floor of headquarters

2512
01:39:06.900 --> 01:39:08.483
and the only Black person.

2513
01:39:09.686 --> 01:39:12.700
So she called herself the double only.

2514
01:39:12.700 --> 01:39:16.500
And she had so much imposter syndrome

2515
01:39:16.500 --> 01:39:19.750
and it was a real challenge for her.

2516
01:39:19.750 --> 01:39:22.010
And also probably thinking that

2517
01:39:22.010 --> 01:39:24.800
she doesn't have the liberty of being able to make mistakes.

2518
01:39:24.800 --> 01:39:27.850
Bingo. Because of being the double only.

2519
01:39:27.850 --> 01:39:29.913
So we talked about this

2520
01:39:29.913 --> 01:39:31.260
and she particularly froze up

2521
01:39:31.260 --> 01:39:33.710
when she was on like the corporate road show.

2522
01:39:33.710 --> 01:39:36.217
So overseas, giving talks about,

2523
01:39:36.217 --> 01:39:38.430
"Hey, here's the vision, here's the strategy,

2524
01:39:38.430 --> 01:39:40.440
here's how we're gonna do it."

2525
01:39:40.440 --> 01:39:41.500
She got really panicked.

2526
01:39:41.500 --> 01:39:44.560
She felt like she wasn't herself on stage.

2527
01:39:44.560 --> 01:39:46.120
So for a while

2528
01:39:46.120 --> 01:39:49.460
we tried just having her pretend that she was performing,

2529
01:39:49.460 --> 01:39:50.660
but that didn't work for her.

2530
01:39:50.660 --> 01:39:51.870
She's like, "But I, that's not me."

2531
01:39:51.870 --> 01:39:53.520
Like, "I'm a genuine person."

2532
01:39:53.520 --> 01:39:55.694
I'm like, "What do you really wanna say?"

2533
01:39:55.694 --> 01:39:56.897
And she looked at me, she's like,

2534
01:39:56.897 --> 01:40:00.820
"What I really wanna say is I have no idea how I got here.

2535
01:40:00.820 --> 01:40:03.190
I'm utterly overwhelmed.

2536
01:40:03.190 --> 01:40:04.720
And this is crazy."

2537
01:40:04.720 --> 01:40:06.077
Like, "Why don't you just say that?"

2538
01:40:06.077 --> 01:40:07.127
And to your point, she's like,

2539
01:40:07.127 --> 01:40:07.960
"I can't say that.

2540
01:40:07.960 --> 01:40:10.470
These people like were a publicly traded company,

2541
01:40:10.470 --> 01:40:12.470
like that'll be on the cover of the Wall Street Journal.

2542
01:40:12.470 --> 01:40:14.337
Like, I don't know how I got here."

2543
01:40:15.680 --> 01:40:16.900
But then I asked her cause she,

2544
01:40:16.900 --> 01:40:18.880
this was after my essay on OCD.

2545
01:40:18.880 --> 01:40:20.223
Like, "Well, you read what I wrote, right?

2546
01:40:20.223 --> 01:40:21.410
That was all over the place."

2547
01:40:21.410 --> 01:40:22.672
She's like, "Yeah.

2548
01:40:22.672 --> 01:40:24.302
I'm like, "Well, how did that make you feel about me?"

2549
01:40:24.302 --> 01:40:25.135
She's like,

2550
01:40:25.135 --> 01:40:25.968
"It's actually like

2551
01:40:25.968 --> 01:40:27.667
part of the reason I wanted to work with you."

2552
01:40:27.667 --> 01:40:30.300
I'm like, "Well, I clearly don't have it all together.

2553
01:40:30.300 --> 01:40:32.600
Like why would you wanna work with me? I'm a mess."

2554
01:40:32.600 --> 01:40:34.730
She's like, "No, like you're a human."

2555
01:40:34.730 --> 01:40:35.563
And I'm like,

2556
01:40:35.563 --> 01:40:36.650
"All right, so you can't say that

2557
01:40:36.650 --> 01:40:38.150
you have no idea what's going on,

2558
01:40:38.150 --> 01:40:40.520
but maybe you can get close to that?"

2559
01:40:40.520 --> 01:40:43.590
So ultimately she ended up going on stage

2560
01:40:43.590 --> 01:40:45.300
and she started to open up these talks

2561
01:40:45.300 --> 01:40:48.180
by saying that, "I feel like an accidental executive.

2562
01:40:48.180 --> 01:40:50.920
Sometimes I walk onto a stage like this

2563
01:40:50.920 --> 01:40:53.170
and I'm in awe of how I got here.

2564
01:40:53.170 --> 01:40:54.827
And it's a really challenging job

2565
01:40:54.827 --> 01:40:56.860
and I'm doing the best that I can.

2566
01:40:56.860 --> 01:40:58.500
And that's why it's so important

2567
01:40:58.500 --> 01:41:00.798
that I need all of your help and engage in the company

2568
01:41:00.798 --> 01:41:01.770
and this, that and the other."

2569
01:41:01.770 --> 01:41:03.520
And then everybody fell in love with her.

2570
01:41:03.520 --> 01:41:04.720
Everybody fell in love with her,

2571
01:41:04.720 --> 01:41:07.830
the reviews after the talks skyrocketed

2572
01:41:07.830 --> 01:41:10.820
and most important is she felt like herself.

2573
01:41:10.820 --> 01:41:12.210
So that's all it took.

2574
01:41:12.210 --> 01:41:13.500
Cause she knew she was.

2575
01:41:13.500 --> 01:41:14.750
When she went up there all corporate,

2576
01:41:14.750 --> 01:41:16.270
like, "These are the vision and values."

2577
01:41:16.270 --> 01:41:18.148
And, "I don't know what I'm talking about."

2578
01:41:18.148 --> 01:41:20.360
And I think that that's another truth

2579
01:41:20.360 --> 01:41:23.230
is that so many of us are making it up as we go.

2580
01:41:23.230 --> 01:41:25.370
In a way we all are. Some of us are just making it up

