Talk to me about out the movement piece.

2835
01:51:10.440 --> 01:51:11.810
Yeah. I mean, you talk to me,

2836
01:51:11.810 --> 01:51:12.930
you know, better than anyone.

2837
01:51:12.930 --> 01:51:14.360
I mean, you know, I don't, you know, it's like, listen,

2838
01:51:14.360 --> 01:51:16.706
we're on the same page here.
We're on the same page here.

2839
01:51:16.706 --> 01:51:18.010
I can't, I can't be,
It's impossible,

2840
01:51:18.010 --> 01:51:21.230
a happy, functional, productive, human being

2841
01:51:21.230 --> 01:51:22.440
unless I'm moving my body

2842
01:51:22.440 --> 01:51:23.450
on the regular-
So this was,

2843
01:51:23.450 --> 01:51:25.876
this was a really interesting conversation with my,

2844
01:51:25.876 --> 01:51:29.810
my editor who I loved, which is

2845
01:51:29.810 --> 01:51:30.990
movement, at first,

2846
01:51:30.990 --> 01:51:34.553
felt disconnected from the other principles in this book,

2847
01:51:34.553 --> 01:51:37.300
at least to a lot of people at my publisher,

2848
01:51:37.300 --> 01:51:38.184
not to me-
Because why?

2849
01:51:38.184 --> 01:51:40.300
Because these other things are these like

2850
01:51:40.300 --> 01:51:44.610
very age-old, psychological principles,

2851
01:51:44.610 --> 01:51:46.230
and this is a perfect way into movement.

2852
01:51:46.230 --> 01:51:47.980
Cause most people, you and I hear movement

2853
01:51:47.980 --> 01:51:50.616
and we think probably more psychology than physiology.

2854
01:51:50.616 --> 01:51:51.449
Sure.

2855
01:51:51.449 --> 01:51:53.850
But most people hear movement and they think exercise,

2856
01:51:53.850 --> 01:51:55.150
they think, "What does exercise have to do

2857
01:51:55.150 --> 01:51:58.197
with vulnerability, or patience, or community?"

2858
01:51:59.202 --> 01:52:01.740
And the argument that I made to them

2859
01:52:01.740 --> 01:52:02.790
and then that I make in the book

2860
01:52:02.790 --> 01:52:05.140
is it actually has everything to do

2861
01:52:05.140 --> 01:52:07.120
with these other attributes.

2862
01:52:07.120 --> 01:52:11.560
So Rene Descartes separated the mind and body.

2863
01:52:11.560 --> 01:52:12.940
And that was a huge mistake.

2864
01:52:12.940 --> 01:52:14.930
And our thinking is still very much

2865
01:52:14.930 --> 01:52:15.763
in this-
Bifurcated body.

2866
01:52:15.763 --> 01:52:18.810
in this bifurcated mind-body thinking,

2867
01:52:18.810 --> 01:52:21.530
when in fact the latest research shows

2868
01:52:21.530 --> 01:52:23.620
that we are not a mind or body,

2869
01:52:23.620 --> 01:52:25.020
we're not even a mind and body:

2870
01:52:25.020 --> 01:52:27.030
we're a mind-body system.

2871
01:52:27.030 --> 01:52:30.780
And it would be intellectually dishonest for me

2872
01:52:30.780 --> 01:52:32.903
to talk about all these other principles and tools

2873
01:52:32.903 --> 01:52:34.860
and not include movement.

2874
01:52:34.860 --> 01:52:36.730
Because before I do any of the other work

2875
01:52:36.730 --> 01:52:38.440
with my coaching clients,

2876
01:52:38.440 --> 01:52:40.070
I help them develop a movement practice

2877
01:52:40.070 --> 01:52:41.640
if they don't have one.

2878
01:52:41.640 --> 01:52:43.970
And a movement practice doesn't

2879
01:52:43.970 --> 01:52:46.460
need to be cycling, or swimming, or CrossFit:

2880
01:52:46.460 --> 01:52:49.940
it can be gardening, it could be dancing, it could be yoga.

2881
01:52:49.940 --> 01:52:51.280
And what I find for most people

2882
01:52:51.280 --> 01:52:53.730
and actually what I do the most for myself right now

2883
01:52:53.730 --> 01:52:55.010
is just walking.

2884
01:52:55.010 --> 01:52:57.940
The benefits of a daily brisk walk

2885
01:52:57.940 --> 01:52:59.730
are just you're-
You're also Jack,

2886
01:52:59.730 --> 01:53:01.600
you're also pretty jacked up, right?

2887
01:53:01.600 --> 01:53:02.772
(general laughter)

2888
01:53:02.772 --> 01:53:04.380
So, so, so the shirt might be tight.

2889
01:53:04.380 --> 01:53:06.107
First thing when you walked in, I was like,

2890
01:53:06.107 --> 01:53:07.620
"Whoa, someone's been in the weight room

2891
01:53:07.620 --> 01:53:09.120
since the last time I saw you."

2892
01:53:09.120 --> 01:53:14.120
So, so I, in the spirit of full disclosure right now,

2893
01:53:14.140 --> 01:53:15.740
my physical practice looks like

2894
01:53:15.740 --> 01:53:18.470
four days a week I strength train for an hour

2895
01:53:18.470 --> 01:53:21.660
and I have a phenomenal coach that is just fun to work with

2896
01:53:21.660 --> 01:53:24.050
and we get all kinds of cool skill development.

2897
01:53:24.050 --> 01:53:25.470
And then every day

2898
01:53:25.470 --> 01:53:28.683
I walk for between 45 minutes and two hours.

2899
01:53:30.043 --> 01:53:34.153
And those are long walks with Ananda, my German Shepherd.

2900
01:53:35.725 --> 01:53:37.197
And the, because you went there

2901
01:53:37.197 --> 01:53:38.120
and because I know

2902
01:53:38.120 --> 01:53:40.480
a large portion of your audience are athletes,

2903
01:53:40.480 --> 01:53:42.890
a big part of the strength training,

2904
01:53:42.890 --> 01:53:45.980
somewhat related to the principles in this book,

2905
01:53:45.980 --> 01:53:49.300
is it gives me something that is tangible and measurable

2906
01:53:49.300 --> 01:53:51.510
where there's concrete improvement,

2907
01:53:51.510 --> 01:53:54.350
that doesn't depend on what other people think.

2908
01:53:54.350 --> 01:53:56.410
So if I put a book out into the world,

2909
01:53:56.410 --> 01:53:59.360
I have no control whether or not people buy it.

2910
01:53:59.360 --> 01:54:02.670
I try as hard as I can to practice what I preach

2911
01:54:02.670 --> 01:54:03.960
and say, "Well, what are my core values?

2912
01:54:03.960 --> 01:54:06.220
What's my internal dashboard? Did the book live up to them?

2913
01:54:06.220 --> 01:54:08.810
What do the people I respect most think of the book?"

2914
01:54:08.810 --> 01:54:10.090
But still it's out there in the world.

2915
01:54:10.090 --> 01:54:12.150
Sales numbers are a thing; they matter.

2916
01:54:12.150 --> 01:54:14.080
Whereas in the weight room I get this thing

2917
01:54:14.080 --> 01:54:16.230
that I can work to make progress at

2918
01:54:16.230 --> 01:54:18.123
that is solely in my control.

2919
01:54:19.510 --> 01:54:21.870
The closest thing to it is like a pool,

2920
01:54:21.870 --> 01:54:23.480
cause like even in running and cycling,

2921
01:54:23.480 --> 01:54:25.267
there's weather conditions change, but like

2922
01:54:25.267 --> 01:54:27.980
45 pounds is 45 pounds, you know,

2923
01:54:27.980 --> 01:54:29.790
a hundred meters is a hundred meters.

2924
01:54:29.790 --> 01:54:31.250
So I think it's important for me,

2925
01:54:31.250 --> 01:54:34.440
it helps me stay grounded, to have this very real thing

2926
01:54:34.440 --> 01:54:37.777
that the successes are a hundred percent concrete

2927
01:54:37.777 --> 01:54:39.347
and can be traced directly to myself

2928
01:54:39.347 --> 01:54:40.950
and the failures are in my face.

2929
01:54:40.950 --> 01:54:41.783
I can't be like,

2930
01:54:41.783 --> 01:54:44.430
"Well, it was a bad news cycle," or, "People don't get it."

2931
01:54:44.430 --> 01:54:47.140
It's like, "No, like I, I didn't hit the weight."

2932
01:54:47.140 --> 01:54:49.860
But it's something that I struggle with all the time.

2933
01:54:49.860 --> 01:54:51.878
It's something I talk about with my therapist all the time

2934
01:54:51.878 --> 01:54:54.440
is, "Am I just addicted to progress?"

2935
01:54:54.440 --> 01:54:57.220
Like, why do I still care how much I squat?

2936
01:54:57.220 --> 01:54:58.880
Why do I need a goal to chase?

2937
01:54:58.880 --> 01:55:00.640
Why can't I just be okay

2938
01:55:00.640 --> 01:55:02.806
walking my dog for an hour and a half?"

2939
01:55:02.806 --> 01:55:04.100
That's a good question.

2940
01:55:04.100 --> 01:55:05.976
It, I spend a lot of time

2941
01:55:05.976 --> 01:55:08.340
tumbling that around in my mind.

2942
01:55:08.340 --> 01:55:09.840
I mean I think

2943
01:55:09.840 --> 01:55:13.020
having a healthy relationship with those numbers

2944
01:55:13.020 --> 01:55:13.853
is important,

2945
01:55:15.740 --> 01:55:18.220
but irrespective of that,

2946
01:55:18.220 --> 01:55:21.730
to be in the habit or the practice of challenging yourself

2947
01:55:21.730 --> 01:55:22.739
to do hard things,

2948
01:55:22.739 --> 01:55:23.618
Yes.

2949
01:55:23.618 --> 01:55:28.000
Is important to developing resilience across the board,

2950
01:55:28.000 --> 01:55:29.920
emotional and physical resilience

2951
01:55:29.920 --> 01:55:33.547
and just the sheer humility that it gives you.

2952
01:55:33.547 --> 01:55:35.600
Yeah. Like, especially when you get older and it's like,

2953
01:55:35.600 --> 01:55:38.270
you can't do the stuff you used to be able to do.

2954
01:55:38.270 --> 01:55:39.570
I think all of that is super healthy.

2955
01:55:39.570 --> 01:55:41.610
And then of course the integrated, you know,

2956
01:55:41.610 --> 01:55:43.730
mind-body system requires it.

2957
01:55:43.730 --> 01:55:45.760
Like, "Emotionally I'm better.

2958
01:55:45.760 --> 01:55:47.740
I'm more present. I feel better.

2959
01:55:47.740 --> 01:55:48.910
I'm happier.

2960
01:55:48.910 --> 01:55:51.030
I just feel more grounded,"

2961
01:55:51.030 --> 01:55:52.696
Yes.
when I'm working out, right?

2962
01:55:52.696 --> 01:55:53.740
And, and that's, but that's movement,

2963
01:55:53.740 --> 01:55:55.133
that's not necessarily connected to a goal,

2964
01:55:55.133 --> 01:55:57.490
But, but that movement can be in the weight room, right?

2965
01:55:57.490 --> 01:55:59.156
Yes.
Oh for sure. It is.

2966
01:55:59.156 --> 01:56:00.530
But what I'm saying is it's like the goal,

2967
01:56:00.530 --> 01:56:03.650
like I always talk about with this guy at the gym, Zach,

2968
01:56:03.650 --> 01:56:04.701
I'm like,

2969
01:56:04.701 --> 01:56:06.470
"You know, at some point I'm just gonna come in here

2970
01:56:06.470 --> 01:56:08.412
to be healthy three days a week."

2971
01:56:08.412 --> 01:56:11.090
I'm not there yet. Like right now I still have a goal.

2972
01:56:11.090 --> 01:56:14.127
Right. And that goal is what just like pushing these PRs?

2973
01:56:14.127 --> 01:56:16.464
Yeah. I mean this, this arbitrary numbers,

2974
01:56:16.464 --> 01:56:20.610
but I'd love to, it's like 5, 4, 3 in power lifting world.

2975
01:56:20.610 --> 01:56:23.230
So it's dead lift 500 pounds, squat 400 pounds

2976
01:56:23.230 --> 01:56:24.820
and bench press 300 pounds.

2977
01:56:24.820 --> 01:56:26.264
But goals add structure

2978
01:56:26.264 --> 01:56:29.226
and build accountability and drive.

2979
01:56:29.226 --> 01:56:31.700
Like in a, in a world in which

2980
01:56:31.700 --> 01:56:35.010
your attention, and time, and energy is limited,

2981
01:56:35.010 --> 01:56:35.950
when you have a goal,

2982
01:56:35.950 --> 01:56:38.396
it helps you prioritize how you focus that attention,

2983
01:56:38.396 --> 01:56:39.560
time, and energy.

2984
01:56:39.560 --> 01:56:40.393
Yes.

2985
01:56:40.393 --> 01:56:41.430
So there's nothing wrong with that

2986
01:56:41.430 --> 01:56:44.320
unless your attachment to that goal

2987
01:56:44.320 --> 01:56:47.120
or the outcome is unhealthy.

2988
01:56:47.120 --> 01:56:49.490
Yeah. And this is something that I wrote about recently.

2989
01:56:49.490 --> 01:56:51.680
So we'll dive in here really quick,

2990
01:56:51.680 --> 01:56:52.760
cause I think it's fascinating

2991
01:56:52.760 --> 01:56:54.480
and maybe you can give me some advice?

2992
01:56:54.480 --> 01:56:56.410
and I don't know if you saw it was just in the newsletter

2993
01:56:56.410 --> 01:56:59.630
so it wasn't like, you know, widely shared on,

2994
01:56:59.630 --> 01:57:02.010
on Outside magazine or anything like that.

2995
01:57:02.010 --> 01:57:05.140
But I recently had a fitness test

2996
01:57:05.140 --> 01:57:08.780
and for the first time in my life,

2997
01:57:08.780 --> 01:57:11.570
there was no part of my identity or self-worth

2998
01:57:11.570 --> 01:57:13.730
that was attached to the result.

2999
01:57:13.730 --> 01:57:16.010
And I couldn't find any extra gear.

3000
01:57:16.010 --> 01:57:18.980
So my relationship with athletics,

3001
01:57:18.980 --> 01:57:22.840
I was bullied when I was in first to third grade,

3002
01:57:22.840 --> 01:57:26.020
really badly, had to switch schools level bullying.

3003
01:57:26.020 --> 01:57:28.470
Then in seventh grade,

3004
01:57:28.470 --> 01:57:30.350
my parents wouldn't let me play football.

3005
01:57:30.350 --> 01:57:31.460
They were concerned about health.

3006
01:57:31.460 --> 01:57:33.810
And I got jumped on the side of the road by high schoolers,

3007
01:57:33.810 --> 01:57:35.580
like beat the crap outta me,

3008
01:57:35.580 --> 01:57:37.870
became super anxious to be outside of my own neighborhood

3009
01:57:37.870 --> 01:57:38.900
because I didn't feel safe.

3010
01:57:38.900 --> 01:57:42.210
Like they, they beat me to the ground.

3011
01:57:42.210 --> 01:57:43.200
Then my parents were like, "All right,

3012
01:57:43.200 --> 01:57:47.120
I guess we'll let Brad play football, for his confidence."

3013
01:57:47.120 --> 01:57:49.230
So I played football and I go in the weight room

3014
01:57:49.230 --> 01:57:52.480
and suddenly my ability to feel safe and confident

3015
01:57:52.480 --> 01:57:54.802
is totally related to my strength.

3016
01:57:54.802 --> 01:57:57.307
And then in high school, girls start liking me

3017
01:57:57.307 --> 01:57:58.530
not because of my brains,

3018
01:57:58.530 --> 01:58:00.090
it's because like the size of my arms

3019
01:58:00.090 --> 01:58:01.890
and I'm the captain of the football team

3020
01:58:01.890 --> 01:58:05.530
and my entire identity is connected to football.

3021
01:58:05.530 --> 01:58:06.990
So when I go to school at Michigan

3022
01:58:06.990 --> 01:58:08.950
and I'm not good enough to play there,

3023
01:58:08.950 --> 01:58:09.783
Right.

3024
01:58:09.783 --> 01:58:12.033
And I can't have anything to do with sports

3025
01:58:12.033 --> 01:58:13.810
with, excuse me, with the sport of football,

3026
01:58:13.810 --> 01:58:16.420
I don't even go to games anymore. It was too painful.

3027
01:58:16.420 --> 01:58:18.490
So I'm just kind of like, and I can't,

3028
01:58:18.490 --> 01:58:19.780
I don't lift for cosmetics.

3029
01:58:19.780 --> 01:58:20.613
That was never me.

3030
01:58:20.613 --> 01:58:22.200
Like it's meaningless. It has to have a goal.

3031
01:58:22.200 --> 01:58:23.550
My identity was so connected

3032
01:58:23.550 --> 01:58:25.350
to strength geared towards football.

3033
01:58:26.320 --> 01:58:28.880
So I kind of get into endurance sports

3034
01:58:28.880 --> 01:58:31.330
and I'm doing it as like a hobby level.

3035
01:58:31.330 --> 01:58:33.513
And then the girl that I dated throughout college,

3036
01:58:33.513 --> 01:58:35.960
dumps me kind of out of the blue.

3037
01:58:35.960 --> 01:58:38.310
And at the time that was really painful. So what do I do?

3038
01:58:38.310 --> 01:58:40.770
I totally numb it with triathlon.

3039
01:58:40.770 --> 01:58:42.880
I just train away all my pain,

3040
01:58:42.880 --> 01:58:43.774
all my insecurity around that.

3041
01:58:43.774 --> 01:58:45.510
Yeah. It's good for that.

3042
01:58:45.510 --> 01:58:47.750
And now triathlon is my identity

3043
01:58:47.750 --> 01:58:49.540
and I care deeply about triathlon.

3044
01:58:49.540 --> 01:58:50.840
Your higher power.

3045
01:58:50.840 --> 01:58:51.828
All of that stuff.

3046
01:58:51.828 --> 01:58:55.600
And for the first time in my life,

3047
01:58:55.600 --> 01:58:57.150
getting back into strength training,

3048
01:58:57.150 --> 01:58:59.670
which the long story made short

3049
01:58:59.670 --> 01:59:02.230
is to get better at triathlon

3050
01:59:02.230 --> 01:59:04.730
would've required training so much.

3051
01:59:04.730 --> 01:59:06.307
And my son was coming along.

3052
01:59:06.307 --> 01:59:08.520
I just like had gotten through the OCD thing.

3053
01:59:08.520 --> 01:59:09.353
It's like, you know,

3054
01:59:09.353 --> 01:59:10.190
"I'm just gonna go back to my roots

3055
01:59:10.190 --> 01:59:11.190
and start strength training again."

3056
01:59:11.190 --> 01:59:14.760
I joke with David Epstein, who wrote "Range," all the time.

3057
01:59:14.760 --> 01:59:18.356
Like my fit is definitely with strength training.

3058
01:59:18.356 --> 01:59:21.600
It took me 10 years to try to run a marathon.

3059
01:59:21.600 --> 01:59:24.210
It's equivalent to what I can squat after like two months.

3060
01:59:24.210 --> 01:59:27.000
So my body just responds to this kind of training better.

3061
01:59:27.000 --> 01:59:28.362
So I get back into strength training,

3062
01:59:28.362 --> 01:59:30.460
but for the first time in my life,

3063
01:59:30.460 --> 01:59:32.560
like I love my wife to death.

3064
01:59:32.560 --> 01:59:34.960
We live in a place where we're happy.

3065
01:59:34.960 --> 01:59:36.320
I've got this dog that I love.

3066
01:59:36.320 --> 01:59:38.490
My son means everything to me.

3067
01:59:38.490 --> 01:59:39.700
My professional identity

3068
01:59:39.700 --> 01:59:41.700
is connected to that book right there.

3069
01:59:41.700 --> 01:59:42.810
Maybe the that's not so healthy,

3070
01:59:42.810 --> 01:59:45.780
but certainly more than athletics.

3071
01:59:45.780 --> 01:59:47.190
And I can't find that extra gear.

3072
01:59:47.190 --> 01:59:49.132
I'm like, I just don't care if I make the lift.

3073
01:59:49.132 --> 01:59:51.640
So that's what I'm exploring now,

3074
01:59:51.640 --> 01:59:54.260
is like, "How do I find that extra gear?"

3075
01:59:54.260 --> 01:59:56.970
Or, "Do I even need to find that extra gear anymore?"

3076
01:59:56.970 --> 01:59:59.470
Cause it used to be, if I missed that squat,

3077
01:59:59.470 --> 02:00:01.540
my entire identity is on the line.

3078
02:00:01.540 --> 02:00:03.580
So of course you figure out a way to make it.

3079
02:00:03.580 --> 02:00:04.413
And now I'm just like,

3080
02:00:04.413 --> 02:00:06.950
"Well I already promised myself a breakfast burrito

3081
02:00:06.950 --> 02:00:07.818
regardless if I make this or not.

3082
02:00:07.818 --> 02:00:10.710
So like, it doesn't really matter."

3083
02:00:10.710 --> 02:00:12.920
Well age will purge that out of you.

3084
02:00:12.920 --> 02:00:13.753
Yeah. In what way?

3085
02:00:13.753 --> 02:00:14.586
Yeah.

3086
02:00:14.586 --> 02:00:16.350
You'll get to a point where that's just not part

3087
02:00:16.350 --> 02:00:18.380
of the conversation-
whether or not

3088
02:00:18.380 --> 02:00:19.450
you're gonna make the thing.

3089
02:00:19.450 --> 02:00:20.283
Right.

3090
02:00:20.283 --> 02:00:23.880
And so one of the things that my wife always says to me

3091
02:00:23.880 --> 02:00:25.110
that's super helpful is

3092
02:00:25.110 --> 02:00:28.040
cause you know, when I was training for Ultraman and all,

3093
02:00:28.040 --> 02:00:30.219
it's very performance metrics oriented.

3094
02:00:30.219 --> 02:00:31.052
Yes.

3095
02:00:31.052 --> 02:00:32.370
And you're carving out a huge amount of time

3096
02:00:32.370 --> 02:00:33.840
and you gotta make every minute count.

3097
02:00:33.840 --> 02:00:36.640
And I super focused on how this was gonna translate

3098
02:00:36.640 --> 02:00:39.980
into a certain result that I cared deeply about.

3099
02:00:39.980 --> 02:00:41.140
It's not about that anymore for me.

3100
02:00:41.140 --> 02:00:45.320
But then if it's not about that, what is it about?

3101
02:00:45.320 --> 02:00:47.190
And how are you finding meaning in it?

3102
02:00:47.190 --> 02:00:49.720
And Julie will say, "You gotta do it for the joy."

3103
02:00:49.720 --> 02:00:52.860
Like that's the, the sustainable energy source, right?

3104
02:00:52.860 --> 02:00:56.133
If you're, if you're, if you're chasing something else,

3105
02:00:57.430 --> 02:00:58.570
then, you know,

3106
02:00:58.570 --> 02:00:59.750
it's not gonna be something

3107
02:00:59.750 --> 02:01:01.700
you're gonna be able to stick with over time.

3108
02:01:01.700 --> 02:01:02.596
Yes.

3109
02:01:02.596 --> 02:01:05.030
And to tie it back to what we talked about for me

3110
02:01:05.030 --> 02:01:06.010
with strength training,

3111
02:01:06.010 --> 02:01:09.310
because it happens inside, it's purely related to community.

3112
02:01:09.310 --> 02:01:12.900
So my sustainability and strength training as I age,

3113
02:01:12.900 --> 02:01:14.510
will be directly related to my,

3114
02:01:14.510 --> 02:01:16.636
doing it with a group of other people

3115
02:01:16.636 --> 02:01:19.400
that I have fun with and I enjoy their company.

3116
02:01:19.400 --> 02:01:20.730
Cause if I were to do it alone,

3117
02:01:20.730 --> 02:01:22.700
like again, it doesn't really matter.

3118
02:01:22.700 --> 02:01:23.533
Right.

3119
02:01:23.533 --> 02:01:28.533
Why is meditation not one of these core principles?

3120
02:01:28.860 --> 02:01:30.700
It's a good question.

3121
02:01:30.700 --> 02:01:31.870
I think that I would,

3122
02:01:31.870 --> 02:01:34.713
I would argue that meditation is baked into all of them.

3123
02:01:36.830 --> 02:01:38.470
I, actually let me reframe.

3124
02:01:38.470 --> 02:01:41.840
I would argue that mindfulness is baked into all of them,

3125
02:01:41.840 --> 02:01:44.360
especially the presence principle,

3126
02:01:44.360 --> 02:01:49.360
but the act of meditation itself is not worthy

3127
02:01:49.640 --> 02:01:51.800
of a principle in a book on groundedness.

3128
02:01:51.800 --> 02:01:53.381
It's a practice that would be

3129
02:01:53.381 --> 02:01:56.770
within the subheading of presence.

3130
02:01:56.770 --> 02:01:57.603
Correct.

3131
02:01:57.603 --> 02:02:00.810
And meditation,

3132
02:02:00.810 --> 02:02:02.890
and I have a practice

3133
02:02:02.890 --> 02:02:04.550
and it's been a wonderful thing,

3134
02:02:04.550 --> 02:02:07.380
especially when I was going through OCD,

3135
02:02:07.380 --> 02:02:09.280
nothing is better exposure therapy

3136
02:02:09.280 --> 02:02:10.710
than sitting with your eyes closed

3137
02:02:10.710 --> 02:02:11.790
and being bombarded by

3138
02:02:11.790 --> 02:02:13.340
these intrusive thoughts and feelings

3139
02:02:13.340 --> 02:02:14.580
and then not being able to do anything.

3140
02:02:14.580 --> 02:02:15.413
Yeah.

3141
02:02:15.413 --> 02:02:18.413
So I'm a huge believer in meditation.

3142
02:02:19.619 --> 02:02:23.640
And meditation, today, has been completely ripped out

3143
02:02:23.640 --> 02:02:27.780
of the ancient wisdom traditions where it originated

3144
02:02:27.780 --> 02:02:31.070
and sold is like a balm for all that ails us.

3145
02:02:31.070 --> 02:02:31.903
When in fact,

3146
02:02:31.903 --> 02:02:34.160
you look at any of the contemplative traditions,

3147
02:02:34.160 --> 02:02:35.630
that meditation was a part of

3148
02:02:37.215 --> 02:02:39.190
and the actual act of sitting with your eyes closed

3149
02:02:39.190 --> 02:02:40.550
and having an anchor,

3150
02:02:40.550 --> 02:02:45.140
tends to be one of five to eight other principles.

3151
02:02:45.140 --> 02:02:48.020
And in Buddhism, which is the tradition here in the West,

3152
02:02:48.020 --> 02:02:51.290
that is most closely associated with meditation,

3153
02:02:51.290 --> 02:02:53.780
it's actually just one of eight parts

3154
02:02:53.780 --> 02:02:56.740
of what the Buddha called the noble eightfold path.

3155
02:02:56.740 --> 02:03:00.733
And meditation was taught in Buddhism after ethics.

3156
02:03:01.710 --> 02:03:05.710
So right action came before right being.

3157
02:03:05.710 --> 02:03:08.530
And I think that meditation itself,

3158
02:03:08.530 --> 02:03:11.100
like your practice, is only as strong as your life.

3159
02:03:11.100 --> 02:03:12.030
So if you think that

3160
02:03:12.030 --> 02:03:14.870
sitting with your eyes closed for 20 minutes a day

3161
02:03:14.870 --> 02:03:18.070
is going to somehow help you find freedom,

3162
02:03:18.070 --> 02:03:19.380
or fulfillment, or enlightenment,

3163
02:03:19.380 --> 02:03:21.230
but then you get off the cushion,

3164
02:03:21.230 --> 02:03:22.600
and your life is like

3165
02:03:22.600 --> 02:03:23.433
Completely ungrounded,
completely ungrounded,

3166
02:03:23.433 --> 02:03:26.410
it's not gonna work.

3167
02:03:26.410 --> 02:03:28.980
So I see it as a practice

3168
02:03:28.980 --> 02:03:31.150
that supports all of these principles.

3169
02:03:31.150 --> 02:03:32.840
Also in Buddhism, Sangha,

3170
02:03:32.840 --> 02:03:34.590
which is the word for community,

3171
02:03:34.590 --> 02:03:36.163
was taught before meditation.

3172
02:03:37.470 --> 02:03:38.890
My favorite parable, cause

3173
02:03:38.890 --> 02:03:41.800
I'm on a little Buddhist kick in the whole book.

3174
02:03:41.800 --> 02:03:44.160
I mentioned that my German Shepherd's named Ananda,

3175
02:03:44.160 --> 02:03:45.410
and here's why.

3176
02:03:45.410 --> 02:03:48.140
So in the Pāli Canon,

3177
02:03:48.140 --> 02:03:51.010
which is one of the oldest living texts

3178
02:03:51.010 --> 02:03:52.957
that we have of Buddhism,

3179
02:03:52.957 --> 02:03:55.530
Ananda was the Buddha's loyal attendant.

3180
02:03:55.530 --> 02:03:57.470
And throughout these scriptures and text

3181
02:03:57.470 --> 02:03:58.800
he's painted as like this

3182
02:03:58.800 --> 02:04:01.980
overly eager, enthusiastic, curious,

3183
02:04:01.980 --> 02:04:05.170
wanting to soak up all the knowledge from the Buddha,

3184
02:04:05.170 --> 02:04:06.240
person.

3185
02:04:06.240 --> 02:04:09.440
And Ananda goes up to the Buddha one day

3186
02:04:09.440 --> 02:04:12.650
and there's tons of repetition in the Pāli Canon,

3187
02:04:12.650 --> 02:04:14.850
but I think it's it serves its purpose.

3188
02:04:14.850 --> 02:04:18.560
So he says, "Buddha, Buddha Blessed One, Blessed One.

3189
02:04:18.560 --> 02:04:21.290
I've just heard that good comradeship,

3190
02:04:21.290 --> 02:04:24.320
good friendship, and good Sangha

3191
02:04:24.320 --> 02:04:27.220
is 50% of the spiritual path.

3192
02:04:27.220 --> 02:04:29.360
Could this possibly be true?"

3193
02:04:29.360 --> 02:04:32.230
And Buddha says, "No Ananda, no, you are mistaken.

3194
02:04:32.230 --> 02:04:37.210
No, good comradeship, good Sangha, good friendship,

3195
02:04:37.210 --> 02:04:40.460
is not 50% of the spiritual path."

3196
02:04:40.460 --> 02:04:42.617
And Ananda looks at the Buddha and says,

3197
02:04:42.617 --> 02:04:45.347
"What is it, Blessed One, what could be true?"

3198
02:04:45.347 --> 02:04:47.227
And the Buddha looks at him and he says,

3199
02:04:47.227 --> 02:04:49.970
"Ananda, good comradeship, good friendship,

3200
02:04:49.970 --> 02:04:52.933
good Sangha is the whole of the spiritual path."

3201
02:04:52.933 --> 02:04:55.890
There was nothing in there about meditation.

3202
02:04:55.890 --> 02:05:00.030
So I think that it's a very powerful tool,

3203
02:05:00.030 --> 02:05:00.863
but it's gotta be-
But meditation,

3204
02:05:00.863 --> 02:05:04.740
meditation being a tool that would be important for Sangha

3205
02:05:04.740 --> 02:05:05.782
and important for

3206
02:05:05.782 --> 02:05:06.740
all these things-
Oh, it's important

3207
02:05:06.740 --> 02:05:07.573
for everything.

3208
02:05:07.573 --> 02:05:08.406
And, and again,

3209
02:05:08.406 --> 02:05:09.872
when I'm at my best, I sit for 20 minutes a day,

3210
02:05:09.872 --> 02:05:12.157
doesn't happen as much with a young kid,

3211
02:05:12.157 --> 02:05:16.390
but it is a extremely powerful tool,

3212
02:05:16.390 --> 02:05:20.690
but I think it needs be situated in a broader framework.

3213
02:05:20.690 --> 02:05:22.060
And what's great about meditation too,

3214
02:05:22.060 --> 02:05:24.160
is we talked in acceptance about self-distancing,

3215
02:05:24.160 --> 02:05:25.190
giving advice to a friend.

3216
02:05:25.190 --> 02:05:26.802
Meditation's a great way to do that.

3217
02:05:26.802 --> 02:05:28.468
Sure. Cause you start to see thoughts,

3218
02:05:28.468 --> 02:05:30.730
feeling, situations in your life,

3219
02:05:30.730 --> 02:05:35.130
from the place of being an observer, not in them.

3220
02:05:35.130 --> 02:05:36.440
And then it's the observing you,

3221
02:05:36.440 --> 02:05:39.180
the more you connect with that, you're essence, your core,

3222
02:05:39.180 --> 02:05:41.640
your spirit, whatever you wanna call it,

3223
02:05:41.640 --> 02:05:43.280
the more that you can start

3224
02:05:43.280 --> 02:05:45.080
to make decisions out of that place.

3225
02:05:45.080 --> 02:05:45.980
Right.

3226
02:05:45.980 --> 02:05:48.610
And the greater capacity you have

3227
02:05:48.610 --> 02:05:51.810
to respond from a place of neutrality

3228
02:05:51.810 --> 02:05:53.950
to all of the stressors in your life.

3229
02:05:53.950 --> 02:05:56.956
Neutrality or perhaps even compassion?

3230
02:05:56.956 --> 02:05:59.163
That's what I get outta meditation.

3231
02:06:00.953 --> 02:06:04.220
Meditators understand this. I didn't when I first heard it.

3232
02:06:04.220 --> 02:06:05.053
And now I think it's

3233
02:06:05.053 --> 02:06:07.283
one of the most beautiful quotes on meditation.

3234
02:06:08.550 --> 02:06:10.463
It's an anonymous source,

3235
02:06:11.514 --> 02:06:12.750
but this woman at the end of her life

3236
02:06:12.750 --> 02:06:15.450
was reflecting on her meditation practice.

3237
02:06:15.450 --> 02:06:18.940
And she said that, "Every itch not scratched

3238
02:06:18.940 --> 02:06:20.890
was a lesson in compassion."

3239
02:06:20.890 --> 02:06:22.900
I'm just chewing on that. Explain that.

3240
02:06:22.900 --> 02:06:25.200
Well, how I interpret that

3241
02:06:25.200 --> 02:06:27.010
is that when you're sitting,

3242
02:06:27.010 --> 02:06:29.650
whether it's a physical itch or a mental itch,

3243
02:06:29.650 --> 02:06:32.023
something comes up and you wanna deal with it.

3244
02:06:33.165 --> 02:06:33.998
And the practice is just to-
Your have to let it,

3245
02:06:33.998 --> 02:06:34.831
plan it.

3246
02:06:34.831 --> 02:06:35.664
Let it

3247
02:06:35.664 --> 02:06:36.497
Right, exactly.

3248
02:06:36.497 --> 02:06:37.330
And learning-
Learning the allowing.

3249
02:06:37.330 --> 02:06:39.150
And learning to let it be there

3250
02:06:39.150 --> 02:06:41.590
helps you become a more compassionate person to yourself,

3251
02:06:41.590 --> 02:06:43.350
to others, because we all

3252
02:06:43.350 --> 02:06:46.860
have all these itches that we wanna scratch all the time.

3253
02:06:46.860 --> 02:06:49.190
And it's so hard to live a life

3254
02:06:49.190 --> 02:06:51.830
where we're bombarded with these things that we wanna fix:

3255
02:06:51.830 --> 02:06:53.080
with loss, with disappointment,

3256
02:06:53.080 --> 02:06:56.380
with all these things, and learning to accept it,

3257
02:06:56.380 --> 02:06:58.380
if that doesn't make you a more compassionate person,

3258
02:06:58.380 --> 02:06:59.460
then like what will?

3259
02:06:59.460 --> 02:07:00.396
Right.

3260
02:07:00.396 --> 02:07:02.830
Let's let's close this thing down

3261
02:07:02.830 --> 02:07:05.710
with some practical takeaways for people.

3262
02:07:05.710 --> 02:07:08.600
So perhaps somebody's been listening to this,

3263
02:07:08.600 --> 02:07:12.190
they're thinking, "Okay, I'm on board, Brad,

3264
02:07:12.190 --> 02:07:13.870
I'm gonna get the book,

3265
02:07:13.870 --> 02:07:16.460
but help me with a few simple things

3266
02:07:16.460 --> 02:07:18.420
that I can begin to incorporate into my life

3267
02:07:18.420 --> 02:07:20.420
to cultivate groundedness."

3268
02:07:21.340 --> 02:07:23.050
So we've talked about these things

3269
02:07:23.050 --> 02:07:24.800
from kind of a 10,000 foot view,

3270
02:07:24.800 --> 02:07:27.560
but, and in the book you have like practices,

3271
02:07:27.560 --> 02:07:29.570
like here's the, here's how you kind of do things.

3272
02:07:29.570 --> 02:07:31.867
So you it's certainly in the book,

3273
02:07:31.867 --> 02:07:33.600
but what are a few of your favorites

3274
02:07:33.600 --> 02:07:36.030
or things that you think could be helpful to somebody

3275
02:07:36.030 --> 02:07:39.990
who's trying to, you know, blaze this path?

3276
02:07:39.990 --> 02:07:42.400
Yeah. Blaze it slowly.

3277
02:07:42.400 --> 02:07:43.880
Right? The patience thing.

3278
02:07:43.880 --> 02:07:45.073
Yeah. No, but blaze it is good.

3279
02:07:45.073 --> 02:07:46.280
It's slow variety.

3280
02:07:46.280 --> 02:07:47.270
It's good to have,

3281
02:07:47.270 --> 02:07:48.440
It's good to have motivation

3282
02:07:48.440 --> 02:07:49.530
As Eliud Kipchoge,

3283
02:07:49.530 --> 02:07:51.110
the greatest runner and

3284
02:07:51.110 --> 02:07:53.550
potentially the greatest athlete of all time,

3285
02:07:53.550 --> 02:07:55.780
talked about his progress,

3286
02:07:55.780 --> 02:07:58.200
and he's literally like the fastest human on earth

3287
02:07:58.200 --> 02:07:59.220
over long distances,

3288
02:07:59.220 --> 02:08:01.330
says he makes progress slowly by slowly.

3289
02:08:01.330 --> 02:08:02.163
Right.

3290
02:08:02.163 --> 02:08:02.996
And you talk about you,

3291
02:08:02.996 --> 02:08:04.440
you write about him rarely

3292
02:08:04.440 --> 02:08:06.317
going beyond 80%-
In his workouts,

3293
02:08:06.317 --> 02:08:07.480
in, in his workouts.

3294
02:08:07.480 --> 02:08:09.660
And, and this notion of like, you know,

3295
02:08:09.660 --> 02:08:13.270
one less rep, or like not going to max out.

3296
02:08:13.270 --> 02:08:14.103
Yeah.

3297
02:08:14.103 --> 02:08:15.170
as being a kind of habit

3298
02:08:15.170 --> 02:08:19.330
of building sustainability into your daily routine.

3299
02:08:19.330 --> 02:08:22.269
So, so yes, I love that.

3300
02:08:22.269 --> 02:08:24.730
And just like the compounding consistency.

3301
02:08:24.730 --> 02:08:26.480
And you've had other guests that talk about that topic

3302
02:08:26.480 --> 02:08:28.383
and have whole books on that topic.

3303
02:08:30.233 --> 02:08:33.640
So a couple of like really tactical things.

3304
02:08:33.640 --> 02:08:37.210
The first is the core values work I think is,

3305
02:08:37.210 --> 02:08:38.743
can be really transformative.

3306
02:08:40.329 --> 02:08:42.080
So it doesn't have to be more than a half an hour, an hour,

3307
02:08:42.080 --> 02:08:46.090
but try to come up with the three to five qualities

3308
02:08:46.090 --> 02:08:47.573
that you really aspire towards, that are you,

3309
02:08:47.573 --> 02:08:48.670
when you're at your best,

3310
02:08:48.670 --> 02:08:51.000
and then define them in concrete terms.

3311
02:08:51.000 --> 02:08:52.680
And then for each of those qualities,

3312
02:08:52.680 --> 02:08:55.660
talk about things that you will do or that you'll stop doing

3313
02:08:55.660 --> 02:08:57.393
that allow you to practice them.

3314
02:08:58.900 --> 02:08:59.790
Something else.

3315
02:08:59.790 --> 02:09:01.644
The three you tell, yeah.

3316
02:09:01.644 --> 02:09:03.900
The three things that you wanna adopt

3317
02:09:03.900 --> 02:09:05.810
and the three things you wanna let go of.

3318
02:09:05.810 --> 02:09:07.750
Yes, in service of that core value.

3319
02:09:07.750 --> 02:09:09.220
So if your core value is vulnerability,

3320
02:09:09.220 --> 02:09:10.767
well, what are you doing that goes away from that?

3321
02:09:10.767 --> 02:09:13.060
And what could you do that works toward that?

3322
02:09:13.060 --> 02:09:15.050
If your core value is love, how do you, again,

3323
02:09:15.050 --> 02:09:16.910
how do you define it? What do you do?

3324
02:09:16.910 --> 02:09:18.077
How do you get from love to,

3325
02:09:18.077 --> 02:09:20.431
"I'm gonna put my phone in the glove compartment of the car

3326
02:09:20.431 --> 02:09:22.027
and leave it in the garage."

3327
02:09:23.840 --> 02:09:26.540
Start trying to thank nondualy.

3328
02:09:26.540 --> 02:09:27.827
So when you catch yourself being like,

3329
02:09:27.827 --> 02:09:29.910
"I need to be self-disciplined,"

3330
02:09:29.910 --> 02:09:33.049
how can you be self-disciplined and self-compassionate?

3331
02:09:33.049 --> 02:09:34.950
How can-
That's yes/and.

3332
02:09:34.950 --> 02:09:36.221
How can you sometimes go fast

3333
02:09:36.221 --> 02:09:38.950
and realize when it's important and to step back

3334
02:09:38.950 --> 02:09:41.843
and be good enough and go a little bit slow, more slowly.

3335
02:09:42.970 --> 02:09:46.228
And then another thing that I think is just, is,

3336
02:09:46.228 --> 02:09:49.743
is really helpful and, and has an immediate effect,

3337
02:09:50.857 --> 02:09:54.170
is try to catch yourself shoulding on yourself

3338
02:09:54.170 --> 02:09:58.640
and try to replace that self-talk with wish or want.

3339
02:09:58.640 --> 02:10:00.610
And that's the whole intervention

3340
02:10:00.610 --> 02:10:02.090
and just watch what happens.

3341
02:10:02.090 --> 02:10:04.041
My coaching clients like,

3342
02:10:04.041 --> 02:10:05.590
"Wait, you mean that that's gonna change things?"

3343
02:10:05.590 --> 02:10:07.220
Like just try it.

3344
02:10:07.220 --> 02:10:09.510
And some of the most perfectionist people

3345
02:10:09.510 --> 02:10:10.773
that are hardest on themselves after a few months,

3346
02:10:10.773 --> 02:10:13.757
they're like, "Wow, like, you're right.

